var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"stable_api/#API-Documentation","page":"API Documentation","text":"This is the stable public API of BAT. Forward/backward compatibility follows Julia's semantic versioning rules.","title":"API Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"stable_api/#Types","page":"API Documentation","text":"Pages = [\"stable_api.md\"]\nOrder = [:type]","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"stable_api/#Functions-and-macros","page":"API Documentation","text":"Pages = [\"stable_api.md\"]\nOrder = [:macro, :function]","title":"Functions and macros"},{"category":"section","location":"stable_api/#Documentation","page":"API Documentation","text":"","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_bgml","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_bgml(\n    likelihood, prior,\n    [algorithm::BAT.MaxDensityAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nEstimate the maximum (log-)likelihood parameter point using Bayesian-Guided Maximum Likelihood (BGML).\n\nBGML runs the optimization algorithm in the transformed space where the transformation is derived from prior and algorithm. Typically it is the space in which prior becomes a standard multivariate normal (or other standard) distribution. The optimization target is purely logdensityof(likelihood). The given prior only informs the choice of parameter space. As a likelihood is invariant under reparameterization, the result is not biased by the choice of prior, provided that prior does not vanish in valid parameter regions of non-negligible likelihood and that the optimizer finds the global maximum of the likelihood within the search space. The numerical result may still depend on prior through the parameterization of the search space and the starting values.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = v, ...)\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add methods to bat_bgml, add methods to bat_bgml_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_bgml"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_convergence","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_convergence(\n    algoutput,\n    [algorithm::ConvergenceTest],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nCheck if an algorithm has converged, based on it's output algoutput\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result, ...)\n\nresult indicates whether algoutput and must either be a Bool or support convert(Bool, result). It should typically contains measures of algorithm convergence, like a convergence value and it's threshold, etc.\n\nResult properties not listed here are algorithm-specific and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add methods to bat_convergence, add methods to bat_convergence_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_convergence"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_default","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_default(f::Base.Callable, argname::Symbol, objectives...)\nbat_default(f::Base.Callable, argname::Val, objectives...)\n\nGet the default value for argument argname of function f to use for objective(s).\n\nobjective(s) are mandatory arguments of function f that semantically constitute it's main objective(s), and that that a good default choice of optional arguments (e.g. choice of algorithm(s), etc.) may depend on. Which arguments are considered to be objectives is function-specific.\n\nFor example:\n\nbat_default(bat_sample, :algorithm, density::PosteriorMeasure) == RandomWalk()\nbat_default(bat_sample, Val(:algorithm), samples::DensitySampleVector) == SystematicResampling()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_default"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_eff_sample_size","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_eff_sample_size(\n    v::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real},AbstractVectorOfSimilarVectors{<:Real}},\n    [algorithm::EffSampleSizeAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nbat_eff_sample_size(\n    smpls::DensitySampleVector,\n    [algorithm::EffSampleSizeAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nEstimate effective sample size estimation for variate series v, resp. density samples smpls, separately for each degree of freedom.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = eff_sample_size, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are algorithm-specific and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add methods to bat_eff_sample_size, add methods to bat_eff_sample_size_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_eff_sample_size"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_findmedian","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_findmedian(\n    samples::DensitySampleVector\n)\n\nThe function computes the median of marginalized samples.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = v, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are algorithm-specific and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add methods to bat_findmedian, add methods to bat_findmedian_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_findmedian"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_findmode","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_findmode(\n    target::BAT.MeasureLike,\n    [algorithm::BAT.AbstractModeEstimator],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nEstimate the global mode of target.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = v,)\n\nwith v the estimated mode variate.\n\nUse evalmeasure instead to obtain an EvaluatedMeasure that carries the mode together with all other evaluation results.\n\nImplementation\n\nbat_findmode uses evalmeasure internally. Do not specialize bat_findmode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_findmode"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_initval","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_initval(\n    target::BAT.MeasureLike,\n    [algorithm::BAT.InitvalAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)::V\n\nbat_initval(\n    target::BAT.MeasureLike,\n    n::Integer,\n    [algorithm::BAT.InitvalAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)::AbstractVector{<:V}\n\nGenerate one or n random initial/starting value(s) suitable for target.\n\nAssuming the variates of target are of type T, returns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = X::AbstractVector{T}, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are algorithm-specific and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add methods to bat_initval, add methods likebat_initval_impl(target::MeasureLike, algorithm::InitvalAlgorithm, context::BATContext)\nbat_initval_impl(target::MeasureLike, n::Integer, algorithm::InitvalAlgorithm, context::BATContext)to bat_initval_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_initval"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_integrate","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_integrate(\n    target::MeasureLike,\n    [algorithm::IntegrationAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nCalculate the integral (evidence) of target.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = X,)\n\nwhere X is the mass estimate, typically a Measurements.Measurement or a logarithmic number type wrapping one (e.g. for nested-sampling evidence estimates).\n\nUse evalmeasure instead to obtain an EvaluatedMeasure that carries the mass estimate together with all other evaluation results.\n\nImplementation\n\nbat_integrate uses evalmeasure internally. Do not specialize bat_integrate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_integrate"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_read","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_read(\n    filename::AbstractString,\n    [key,]\n    [algorithm::BATIOAlgorithm]\n)\n\nRead data (optionally selected by key) from filename using algorithm.\n\nExample:\n\nsmpls = bat_read(\"samples.hdf5\", smpls).result\n\nReturns (result = content, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are specific to the output algorithm and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nSee bat_write.\n\nCurrently supported file formats are:\n\nHDF5 with file extension \".h5\" or \".hdf5\"\n\nnote: Note\nHDF5 I/O functionality is only available when the HDF5 package is loaded (e.g. via import HDF5).\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add algorithms to bat_read, add algorithms to bat_read_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_read"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_sample","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_sample(\n    target::BAT.MeasureLike,\n    [algorithm::BAT.AbstractSamplingAlgorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nDraw samples from target using algorithm.\n\nDepending on sampling algorithm, the samples may be independent or correlated (e.g. when using MCMC).\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = X::DensitySampleVector,)\n\nUse evalmeasure instead to obtain an EvaluatedMeasure that carries the samples together with all other evaluation results.\n\nImplementation\n\nbat_sample uses evalmeasure internally. Do not specialize bat_sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_sample"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_write","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_write(\n    filename::AbstractString,\n    content,\n    [algorithm::BATIOAlgorithm]\n)\n\nWrite content to file filename using algorithm.\n\nExample:\n\nsmpls = bat_sample(posterior, ...).result\nbat_write(\"samples.hdf5\", smpls)\n\nReturns (result = filename, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are specific to the output algorithm and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nSee bat_read.\n\nCurrently supported file formats are:\n\nHDF5 with file extension \".h5\" or \".hdf5\"\n\nnote: Note\nHDF5 I/O functionality is only available when the HDF5 package is loaded (e.g. via import HDF5).\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add algorithms to bat_write, add algorithms to bat_write_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_write"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.bat_transform","page":"API Documentation","text":"bat_transform(\n    how::TransformIntent,\n    object,\n    [algorithm::TransformAlgorithm]\n)\n\nbat_transform(\n    f,\n    object,\n    [algorithm::TransformAlgorithm]\n)\n\nTransform object to another variate space: either as implied by the TransformIntent how together with object, or using a given invertible transformation function f directly.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = newdensity, f_transform = vartrafo::Function, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are algorithm-specific and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nnote: Note\nAs a convenience,flat_smpls, f_flatten = bat_transform(Vector, measure)\nflat_smpls, f_flatten = bat_transform(Vector, samples)can be used to flatten a the variate type of a measure (res. samples of a measure) to something like Vector{<:Real}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.evalmeasure","page":"API Documentation","text":"evalmeasure(\n    target::Union{AbstractMeasure,Distribution,DensitySampleVector},\n    [algorithm],\n    [context::BATContext]\n)::EvaluatedMeasure\n\nEvaluate measure or probability distribution target using algorithm and return an EvaluatedMeasure.\n\nIf no algorithm is given, a default will be chosen depending on the type of target. Typically, this will be an algorithm that draws (correlated or uncorrelated) samples from target, and may also yield an approximation of target and other estimates.\n\nImplementation\n\nevalmeasure internally runs evalmeasure_impl. Do not specialize evalmeasure directly, specialize evalmeasure_impl instead to implement new algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.evalmeasure"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.empiricalof","page":"API Documentation","text":"empiricalof(m)::Union{DensitySampleMeasure,Nothing}\n\nGet the empirical measure, based on samples drawn from measure-like object m, associated with m, or nothing if no empirical representation is available. Also see EvaluatedMeasure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.empiricalof"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.samplesof","page":"API Documentation","text":"samplesof(m)::Union{DensitySampleVector,Nothing}\n\nGet the samples associated with measure-like object m, or nothing if no samples are available.\n\nThe returned object is live internal data of m, it must not be modified. Use DensitySampleVector(m) or convert(DensitySampleVector, m) to obtain an independent copy from a DensitySampleMeasure or an EvaluatedMeasure with empirical samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.samplesof"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.approxof","page":"API Documentation","text":"approxof(m)::Union{AbstractMeasure,Nothing}\n\nGet an approximation of measure-like object m, or nothing if no approximation is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.approxof"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.samplegenof","page":"API Documentation","text":"samplegenof(m)::Union{BAT.AbstractSampleGenerator,Nothing}\n\nGet the sample generation scheme associated with measure-like object m, or nothing if none has been computed. The contents of sample generators is algorithm-specific and not part of the stable API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.samplegenof"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.getess","page":"API Documentation","text":"getess(m)::Union{Real,Nothing}\n\nGet the (scalar) effective sample size associated with measure-like object m, or nothing if unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.getess"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.evalinfo","page":"API Documentation","text":"evalinfo(m)::Union{BAT.MeasureEvalInfo,Nothing}\n\nGet information on the (last) evaluation step that generated or updated measure-like object m, or nothing if no such information is available. The contents of evaluation information is algorithm-specific and not part of the stable API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.evalinfo"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.get_batcontext","page":"API Documentation","text":"get_batcontext()::BATContext\n\nGets the current default computational context for BAT.\n\nNote: get_batcontext() does not have a stable return type. Code that needs type stability should pass a context to algorithms explicitly. BAT algorithms that call other algorithms must forward their context automatically, so context is always type stable within nested BAT algorithms.\n\nSee BATContext, set_batcontext and default_batcontext.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.get_batcontext"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.set_batcontext","page":"API Documentation","text":"set_batcontext(new_context::BATContext)\n\nset_batcontext(;\n    precision = ...,\n    rng = ...,\n    cunit = ...,\n    ad = ...,\n    visualizer = ...\n)\n\nSets the default computational context for BAT.\n\nThe new context becomes the process-wide default, visible to all tasks. Options that are not given are taken from the current context, and unsupported keywords are rejected rather than silently ignored. Note that a BATVisualizer that has already been used by a sampling run cannot be reused, so a process-wide default visualizer serves a single run. To override the default for a dynamic scope only, bind default_batcontext via ScopedSettings.with instead - set_batcontext throws when called inside such a scope, as the scoped binding would shadow the assignment anyway.\n\nSee BATContext and get_batcontext.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.set_batcontext"},{"category":"constant","location":"stable_api/#BAT.default_batcontext","page":"API Documentation","text":"default_batcontext::ScopedSettings.ScopedSetting{BATContext}\n\nHolds the default computational context for BAT.\n\nUnless set to a concrete BATContext object, each access default_batcontext[] yields a fresh BATContext() that includes a random number generator is seeded from Random.default_rng() (so Random.seed! makes BAT results reproducible).\n\nset_batcontext(context) or default_batcontext[] = context) set specific BATContext process-wide. default_batcontext[] = ScopedSettings.default_value restores the default.\n\nA context can also be bound for a dynamic scope only, inherited by tasks started within that scope:\n\nusing ScopedSettings: with\n\nwith(default_batcontext => BATContext(ad = ForwardDiff)) do\n    bat_sample(target, MCMCSampling())\nend\n\nAlso see get_batcontext and set_batcontext.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.default_batcontext"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.log_batdebug","page":"API Documentation","text":"log_batdebug(enable::Bool = true)\n\nEnable/disable debug-level logging for BAT and all BAT package extensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.log_batdebug"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.distbind","page":"API Documentation","text":"distbind(f_k, dist, ::typeof(merge))\n\nPerforms a generalized monadic bind, in the functional programming sense, with a transition kernel f_k, a distribution dist, using merge to control the type of \"flattening\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.distbind"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.distprod","page":"API Documentation","text":"distprod(;a = some_dist, b = some_other_dist, ...)\ndistprod(();a = some_dist, b = some_other_dist, ...))\ndistprod([dist1, dist2, dist2, ...])\n\nGenerate a product of distributions, returning either a distribution that has NamedTuples as variates, or arrays as variates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.distprod"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.joint_likelihood","page":"API Documentation","text":"joint_likelihood(likelihoods...)\n\nCombine several likelihoods over a common parameter space into a joint likelihood.\n\nAll component likelihoods are evaluated at the same (i.e. shared) parameter point. The log-density of the joint likelihood is the sum of the component log-densities.\n\nThe components may be given in any form that can serve as a likelihood in a PosteriorMeasure and are converted accordingly.\n\nMeasureBase.insupport is only defined for the joint likelihood if it is defined for all of its components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.joint_likelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.lbqintegral","page":"API Documentation","text":"lbqintegral(integrand, measure)\nlbqintegral(likelihood, prior)\n\nReturns an object that represents the Lebesgue integral over a function in respect to s reference measure. It is also the non-normalized posterior measure that results from integrating the likelihood of a given observation in respect to a prior measure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.lbqintegral"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AbstractMCMCWeightingScheme","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type AbstractMCMCWeightingScheme{T<:Real}\n\nAbstract class for weighting schemes for MCMC samples.\n\nWeight values will have type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractMCMCWeightingScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AbstractPosteriorMeasure","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type AbstractPosteriorMeasure <: BATMeasure end\n\nAbstract type for posterior probability densities.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractPosteriorMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.TransformIntent","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type TransformIntent\n\nAbstract type for variate space transformation intents.\n\nA TransformIntent, together with an object to be transformed, implies a concrete transformation function; the same intent and object always yield the same transformation. Implementations must derive the transformation from the intent and the object alone, and must support meaningful equality comparison (value-carrying intent types must specialize Base.:(==) accordingly; singleton intent types get this for free).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.TransformIntent"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AdaptiveAffineTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct AdaptiveAffineTuning <: MCMCTransformTuning\n\nAdaptive cycle-based MCMC tuning strategy.\n\nAdapts an affine space transformation based on the acceptance ratio and covariance of the previous samples.\n\nThe cycle-based scale and acceptance-window scheme follows the BAT heritage implementation, see O. Schulz et al., \"BAT.jl: A Julia-Based Tool for Bayesian Inference\" (2021).\n\nConstructors:\n\nAdaptiveAffineTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nλ::Float64: Controls the weight given to new covariance information in adapting the     affine transform. Default: 0.5\nβ::Float64: Controls how much the scale of the affine transform is     widened/narrowed depending on the current MH acceptance ratio. Default: 1.5\nc::IntervalSets.ClosedInterval{Float64}: Interval for allowed scale of the affine transform distribution. Default: ClosedInterval(0.0001, 100.0)\nr::Real: Reweighting factor. Take accumulated sample statistics of previous     tuning cycles into account with a relative weight of r. Set to     0 to completely reset sample statistics between each tuning cycle.     Must be finite and nonnegative; positive scaling must remain representable     in the statistics' floating-point precision. Default: 0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AdaptiveAffineTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AdaptiveMultiPropTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct AdaptiveMultiPropTuning <: MCMCProposalTuning\n\nTuning Algorithm for multiple MCMC Proposals. Works by adjusting the picking rule for the proposals to match the individual desired target acceptance rates based on the respective observed acceptance rates.\n\nConstructors:\n\nAdaptiveMultiPropTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nalpha::Float64: Default: 0.1\nbeta::Float64: Default: 0.5\npicking_socket::Float64: Default: 0.8\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AdaptiveMultiPropTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AdaptiveTransformChain","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct AdaptiveTransformChain <: AbstractAdaptiveTransform\n\nA chain of adaptive space transformations, applied innermost first: x = f[end](...f[1](z)...). Tuned via MultiTrafoTuning, with one transform tuning per component.\n\nNote: target-moment-based initializations (like PriorApproxTransformInit) are only exact for the outermost component; inner components should typically use BAT.UnitTransformInit.\n\nConstructors:\n\nAdaptiveTransformChain(f::Tuple{Vararg{AbstractAdaptiveTransform}})\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AdaptiveTransformChain"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AssumeConvergence","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct AssumeConvergence <: ConvergenceTest\n\nNo-op convergence algorithm for bat_convergence, will always declare convergence.\n\nConstructors:\n\nAssumeConvergence(converged::Bool = true)\n\nFields:\n\nconverged::Bool: Default: true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AssumeConvergence"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AutocorLenAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type AutocorLenAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for integrated autocorrelation length estimation algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AutocorLenAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.BATContext","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct BATContext{T}\n\nSet the default computational context for BAT.\n\nConstructors:\n\nBATContext{T}(rng::AbstractRNG, cunit::AbstractComputeUnit, ad::ADSelector)\n\nBATContext{T}(rng::AbstractRNG, cunit::AbstractComputeUnit, ad::ADSelector, visualizer::BATVisualizer)\n\nBATContext(;\n    precision::Type{<:AbstractFloat} = ...,\n    rng::AbstractRNG = ...,\n    cunit::HeterogeneousComputing.AbstractComputeUnit = ...,\n    ad::Union{AutoDiffOperators.ADSelector, Module, Symbol, Val} = ...,\n    visualizer::BATVisualizer = ...,\n)\n\nThe default rng is seeded from Random.default_rng(), so results become reproducible via Random.seed!.\n\nConstructors that omit visualizer produce a context without sampling visualization, see BATVisualizer.\n\nSee get_batcontext, set_batcontext and default_batcontext.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATContext"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.BATHDF5IO","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct BATHDF5IO <: BATIOAlgorithm\n\nSelects the BAT HDF5 format as the output format.\n\nSee bat_write and bat_read.\n\nConstructors:\n\nBATHDF5IO()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATHDF5IO"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.BATIOAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BATIOAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for density transformation algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATIOAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.BinningAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BinningAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for binning algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BinningAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.BrooksGelmanConvergence","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct BrooksGelmanConvergence <: ConvergenceTest\n\nBrooks-Gelman maximum R^2 convergence test.\n\nSee S. P. Brooks and A. Gelman, \"General Methods for Monitoring Convergence of Iterative Simulations\" (1998).\n\nConstructors:\n\nBrooksGelmanConvergence(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nthreshold::Float64: Default: 1.1\ncorrected::Bool: Default: false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BrooksGelmanConvergence"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.CuhreIntegration","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct CuhreIntegration <: IntegrationAlgorithm\n\nCuhreIntegration integration algorithm.\n\nSee T. Hahn, \"Cuba - a library for multidimensional numerical integration\" (2005).\n\nConstructors:\n\nCuhreIntegration(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: UniformBased()\nrtol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:RTOL))\natol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:ATOL))\nminevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MINEVALS))\nmaxevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MAXEVALS))\nkey::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:KEY))\nnthreads::Int64: Default: Base.Threads.nthreads()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the Cuba package is loaded (e.g. via import CUBA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.CuhreIntegration"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.DensitySample","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct DensitySample\n\nA weighted sample drawn according to an statistical density, e.g. a BAT.MeasureLike.\n\nConstructors:\n\nDensitySampleVector(v::Any, logd::Real, weight::Real, info::Any, aux::Any)\n\nFields:\n\nv::Any: variate value\nlogd::Real: log(density) value at v\nweight::Real: Weight of the sample\ninfo::Any: Additional info on the provenance of the sample. Content depends     on the sampling algorithm.\naux::Any: Custom user-defined information attached to the sample.\n\nUse DensitySampleVector to store vectors of multiple samples with an efficient column-based memory layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DensitySample"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.DensitySampleMeasure","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct DensitySampleMeasure{P,T<:Real,W<:Real,...} <: BATMeasure\n\nRepresents an Empirical Measure based on a sample of points (of type P with weights of type W) drawn from a normalizable measure, with the log-density values (of type T) of that measure at the sample points stored as well.\n\nThe sample need not have been drawn in a true IID fashion, but may also be the result of MCMC and other sampling methods.\n\nA DensitySampleMeasure can be converted to an independent DensitySampleVector copy.\n\nThe measure snapshots the sampling weights at construction and builds a private cumulative distribution. Its values, log densities, info, and aux columns remain shared with smpls, but its sampling weights do not. Later weight changes require constructing a replacement DensitySampleMeasure. In particular, the live data returned by samplesof must not be modified: mutating its owned weights would desynchronize its cached sampling CDF.\n\nThe stored effective sample size (ess) records sampling-process provenance, not empirical-measure content. It is available through getess, but does not participate in equality or hashing.\n\nNote: DensitySampleMeasure does not support logdensityof. An empirical measure has no density in the usual sense, the log-density values of the original measure at the sample points are available via samplesof(dsm).logd.\n\nConstructors:\n\nfunction DensitySampleMeasure(\n    smpls::DensitySampleVector;\n    dof::Union{IntegerLike,Nothing} = nothing,\n    ess::Union{RealLike,Nothing} = nothing,\n    mass::Union{RealLike,MeasureBase.AbstractUnknownMass} = 1,\n)\n\nA DensitySampleMeasure has mass one by default, as the measure the samples were drawn from is treated as implicitly normalized, even if it was a scaled probability measure of possibly unknown total mass (e.g. a non-normalized Bayesian posterior measure).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DensitySampleMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.DensitySampleVector","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct DensitySampleVector <: AbstractVector{<:DensitySample}\n\nA vector of DensitySample elements.\n\nDensitySampleVector is currently a type alias for StructArrays.StructArray{<:DensitySample,...}, though this is subject to change without deprecation.\n\nConstructors:\n\nfunction DensitySampleVector(;\n    v::AbstractVector,\n    logd::AbstractVector{<:Real},\n    weight::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Symbol},\n    info::AbstractVector,\n    aux::AbstractVector\n)\n\nDensitySampleVector(\n    (\n        v::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Real}},\n        logd::AbstractVector{<:Real},\n        weight::AbstractVector{<:Real},\n        info::AbstractVector{<:Any},\n        aux::AbstractVector{<:Any}\n    )\n)\n\nWith weight = :multiplicity repeated samples will be replaced by a single sample, with a weight equal to the number of repetitions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DensitySampleVector"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.DivonneIntegration","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct DivonneIntegration <: IntegrationAlgorithm\n\nDivonneIntegration integration algorithm.\n\nSee T. Hahn, \"Cuba - a library for multidimensional numerical integration\" (2005).\n\nConstructors:\n\nDivonneIntegration(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: UniformBased()\nrtol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:RTOL))\natol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:ATOL))\nminevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MINEVALS))\nmaxevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MAXEVALS))\nkey1::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:KEY1))\nkey2::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:KEY2))\nkey3::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:KEY3))\nmaxpass::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MAXPASS))\nborder::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:BORDER))\nmaxchisq::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MAXCHISQ))\nmindeviation::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MINDEVIATION))\nngiven::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NGIVEN))\nldxgiven::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:LDXGIVEN))\nnextra::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NEXTRA))\nnthreads::Int64: Default: Base.Threads.nthreads()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the Cuba package is loaded (e.g. via import CUBA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DivonneIntegration"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.DoNotTransform","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct DoNotTransform <: TransformIntent\n\nThe identity density transformation target, specifies that densities should not be transformed.\n\nConstructors:\n\nDoNotTransform()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DoNotTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.DriftCommitSchedule","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct DriftCommitSchedule\n\nTransform-installation policy of FisherTransformTuning.\n\nGeometry statistics accumulate continuously, but a new transformation is committed only when the estimated geometry has drifted far enough from the installed one: their distance in the affine-invariant SPD metric must exceed commit_threshold plus a statistical noise floor.\n\nEarly in warmup, noisy estimates drift fast and commits are frequent; as the estimate converges, commits cease on their own - there are no scheduled adaptation windows.\n\nConstructors:\n\nDriftCommitSchedule(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ncommit_threshold::Float64: Commit threshold in the affine-invariant SPD metric (a statistical     noise floor is added automatically). Default: 0.3\ncheck_interval::Int64: Steps between drift evaluations. Default: 10\nmemory_length::Int64: Steps per foreground-background estimator memory block, 0 selects     an automatic, dimension-derived length. Default: 0\nmin_observations::Int64: Minimum number of accumulated observations before the first commit,     0 selects an automatic, dimension-derived count. Default: 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DriftCommitSchedule"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.EffSampleSizeAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type EffSampleSizeAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for integrated autocorrelation length estimation algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.EffSampleSizeAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.EffSampleSizeFromAC","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct EffSampleSizeFromAC <: EffSampleSizeAlgorithm\n\nEffective sample size estimation based on the integrated autocorrelation length of the samples - a property of the ordered sampling process.\n\nFor uniformly weighted samples the stored order is taken as the process order. Samples carrying MCMC sample ids are decomposed into their exact per-walker ordered sequences (repetition weights are expanded exactly), whose independent ESS contributions are pooled by their weight-mass fractions. For nonuniformly weighted samples without process provenance, a resample-then-autocorrelate heuristic is used - KishESS is the provenance-free alternative (and the default in that case).\n\nA singleton has ESS one. ESS stays between one and the stored draw count. Constant, nonpositive, and antithetic autocorrelation lengths reach the upper bound. Non-finite data or estimates raise ArgumentError. Integer data uses widened centering before the FFT.\n\nConstructors:\n\nEffSampleSizeFromAC(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nacalg::AutocorLenAlgorithm: Default: GeyerAutocorLen()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.EffSampleSizeFromAC"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.EvaluatedMeasure","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct EvaluatedMeasure <: BATMeasure\n\nCombines a measure with samples and other information on it.\n\nConstructors:\n\nem = EvaluatedMeasure(\n    measure;\n    transform_intent = ..., f_transform = ..., empirical = ..., approx = ...,\n    dof = ..., mass = ..., modes = ..., samplegen = ..., transformed = ...,\n    evalinfo = ...\n)\n\nBAT.unevaluated(em) === BAT.unevaluated(batmeasure(measure))\n\nunevaluated(em) returns the original measure.\n\nIf measure is itself an EvaluatedMeasure, the keyword arguments update its content: given values replace the corresponding entries, ScopedSettings.unchanged (the default) keeps them, and nothing (resp. MeasureBase.UnknownMass() for mass) clears them.\n\nAn EvaluatedMeasure maintains at most one transformed-space view of its content, identified by transform_intent. Every transformed side of its BAT.BispacedMeasure entries is the representation in that flat transformed space. transform_intent === DoNotTransform() means that no view exists, f_transform is identity then by convention. The constructor checks that supplied transformed-space content is compatible with the view and rejects it with an error otherwise (see the extended help for details).\n\nProperties:\n\nunevaluated: The original measure, as a BAT.BispacedMeasure: em.unevaluated.main is the bare measure itself (returned by unevaluated(em)), em.unevaluated.transformed may cache the bare measure in the transformed space. The cache preserves object identity across repeated evaluations (keeping compiled artifacts like AD preparations valid); it is derived purely from the measure and transform_intent, so any copy is equally valid.\ntransform_intent: The TransformIntent that identifies the transformed space of this measure's content.\nf_transform: The concrete transformation function of the view, mapping variates of the measure to the flat transformed space. Cached with the same identity-preservation rationale as the unevaluated cache. identity if no view exists, nothing if not cached.\nempirical: A BAT.BispacedMeasure that holds a DensitySampleMeasure based on samples drawn from the measure, possibly together with a row-aligned representation of the same samples in the transformed space, or nothing if no samples are available.\napprox: A BAT.BispacedMeasure that holds an approximation of the measure, possibly together with a representation of the same approximation in the transformed space, or nothing if no approximation is available. An approximation captures the shape of the measure, not its total mass: approximations are typically probability measures, like a normal distribution under a normalizing flow or a normalized mixture, while the measure itself is often non-normalized. Total-mass knowledge about the measure lives in mass.\ndof: The degrees of freedom of the measure, or nothing if unknown.\nmass: The mass of the measure, or a MeasureBase.AbstractUnknownMass if unknown.\nmodes: The modes of the measure, or nothing if unknown.\nsamplegen: An object that carries the information needed to generate further samples, or nothing if no sample generation scheme has been computed. Its contents are algorithm-specific and not part of the stable API. Like all transformed-space content it operates in the flat, unshaped transformed space, or in the unshaped space of the measure itself if no view exists. Consumers must not mutate it, continuing sample generation requires a deep copy. It is in principle independent of empirical (evalinfo records what produced the current empirical content), but for now, algorithms that replace the empirical content without using the stored scheme (like resampling and i.i.d. sampling) clear it conservatively.\nevalinfo: Information on the (last) evaluation step that generated/updated this measure, or nothing if no evaluation has been performed or information on it is not available.\n\nThe transform_intent keyword switches the transformed-space view: ScopedSettings.unchanged means that any given transformed-space content refers to the current view. A differing intent adopts the given content and strips the transformed-space sides off all entries that are kept, including the cached transformation function unless a new f_transform is given with it. The transformed keyword updates the transformed-space cache of unevaluated with a bare measure, nothing drops the cache.\n\nExtended help\n\nThe transformed-space view is defined by the contract\n\nunevaluated.transformed, f_transform == transform_and_unshape(transform_intent, unevaluated.main)\nempirical.main == bat_transform(inverse(f_transform), empirical.transformed).result\n\nEquality here is by value, up to floating-point roundtrip through the inverse in the second line. The sample rows of the two empirical sides are aligned and have identical weights, the approximation pair satisfies the corresponding pushforward relation, and when f_transform is not cached the equations are understood relative to the implied transformation. The stored entries preserve the object identity of one evaluation of the pure right-hand sides, whose values do not depend on the evaluation context.\n\nThe transformed side of a BAT.BispacedMeasure pair carries the hash of the transformation it was produced under, and the constructor checks that hash against the (possibly updated) f_transform of the view. Pairs are adopted exactly when their transformed-space content is compatible with the view and rejected with an error otherwise, never silently mislabeled (up to hash collisions). This includes pairs taken from another EvaluatedMeasure of the same measure, like EvaluatedMeasure(em1, empirical = em2.empirical). The transformed and samplegen entries carry no such witness and must be accompanied by an explicit transform_intent in the same update.\n\nThe hash witnesses only that the sides of a pair are connected by the view's transformation. That the main-side content itself belongs to the measure is the responsibility of whoever supplies it, exactly as when supplying raw samples. Hashes of transformation types without a value-based hash specialization are session-bound, so their pairs are rejected after deserialization. Strip the stale view via transform_intent = DoNotTransform() and re-evaluate to recover. Use BAT.validate_evalmeasure to verify the full transformed-space-view contract explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.EvaluatedMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.ExplicitInit","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct ExplicitInit <: InitvalAlgorithm\n\nUses initial values from a given vector of one or more values/variates. The values are used in the order they appear in the vector, not randomly.\n\nConstructors:\n\nExplicitInit(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nxs::AbstractVector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ExplicitInit"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.FisherTransformTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct FisherTransformTuning <: MCMCTransformTuning\n\nTunes MCMC space transformations for gradient-based proposals (currently HamiltonianMC and MALAProposal) by minimizing the empirical Fisher divergence of the transformed target to a standard normal distribution (following A. Seyboldt, E. L. Carlson and B. Carpenter, \"Preconditioning Hamiltonian Monte Carlo by minimizing Fisher Divergence\" (2026)).\n\nFor an affine transformation x = A z + μ with G = A Aᵀ, the optimum satisfies G Cov(α) G = Cov(x), where α = ∇x log(target) is the target score - the affine-invariant geometric mean of the position covariance and the inverse score covariance. For sufficiently regular targets (vanishing boundary terms) the score has zero mean and Cov(α) = E[-∇²log(target)], the average local curvature. For a Gaussian target Cov(x) = Σ while Cov(α) = Σ⁻¹, so the optimum is G = Σ. The z-space gradients that these proposals compute are mapped back through the current transformation, so no additional density or gradient evaluations are required.\n\nPositions and scores are accumulated in the fixed pre-adaptive space with foreground-background memory (early, transient-contaminated draws are periodically forgotten). Transform updates follow the schedule; each committed transform restarts dual averaging in the step-size adaptor (see BAT.StepSizeAdaptor). HMC first searches for a reasonable step size in the new geometry, whereas MALA restarts around its current τ.\n\nFisher moment, fit, and validation state uses the chain's floating-point type.\n\nConstructors:\n\nFisherTransformTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nschedule::Any: Transform-installation policy. Default: DriftCommitSchedule()\nregularization::Float64: Regularization added to the diagonal of both covariance estimates,     relative to their mean variance scale. Default: 1.0e-5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.FisherTransformTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.FixedMGVISchedule","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type FixedMGVISchedule <: BAT.MGVISchedule\n\nAbstract supertype for MGVI sampling schedules.\n\nConstructors:\n\nFixedMGVISchedule(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnsamples::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Default: range(12, 1000, length = 10)\n\nConstructors:\n\nFixedMGVISchedule(nsamples::AbstractVector{<:Real}): The number of samples to draw at each MGVI step. The length of nsamples implies the total number of steps. The number of samples will be rounded to integer values if necessary, to allow for constructions like FixedMGVISchedule(range(12, 1000, length = 10)).\n\nFields:\n\nnsamples::AbstractVector{<:Real}: See constructor above.\n\nSee MGVISampling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.FixedMGVISchedule"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.FixedNBins","page":"API Documentation","text":"FixedNBins(nbins::Int)\n\nSelects a fixed number of bins.\n\nConstructor: FixedNBins(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnbins::Int64: Default: 200\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.FixedNBins"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.FreedmanDiaconisBinning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct FreedmanDiaconisBinning <: BinningAlgorithm\n\nSelects automatic binning based on the Freedman–Diaconis rule.\n\nSee D. Freedman and P. Diaconis, \"On the histogram as a density estimator: L2 theory\" (1981).\n\nConstructor: FreedmanDiaconisBinning()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.FreedmanDiaconisBinning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.GelmanRubinConvergence","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct GelmanRubinConvergence <: ConvergenceTest\n\nGelman-Rubin maximum R^2 convergence test.\n\nSee A. Gelman and D. B. Rubin, \"Inference from Iterative Simulation Using Multiple Sequences\" (1992).\n\nConstructors:\n\nGelmanRubinConvergence(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nthreshold::Float64: Default: 1.1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.GelmanRubinConvergence"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.GeyerAutocorLen","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct GeyerAutocorLen <: AutocorLenAlgorithm\n\nIntegrated autocorrelation length estimation based on Geyer’s initial monotone sequence criterion\n\nSee C. J. Geyer, \"Practical Markov Chain Monte Carlo\" (1992) and C. J. Geyer, \"Introduction to Markov Chain Monte Carlo\" (2011).\n\nConstructors:\n\nGeyerAutocorLen()\n\nThe same algorithm is used by STAN (v2.21) and MCMCChains.jl (v3.0, function ess_rhat).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.GeyerAutocorLen"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.HamiltonianMC","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct HamiltonianMC <: MCMCProposal\n\nThe Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) sampling algorithm, using the multinomial no-U-turn sampler (NUTS) to determine trajectory lengths dynamically.\n\nSee M. Betancourt, \"A Conceptual Introduction to Hamiltonian Monte Carlo\" (2017).\n\nThe Hamiltonian uses an identity mass matrix. Instead of adapting a mass matrix, BAT tunes the MCMC space transformation (see the transform_tuning option of TransformedMCMC); for affine transformations this is mathematically equivalent to adapting a constant metric, and the transformation view extends to nonlinear transports. Trajectory tuning is limited to the leapfrog step size (see BAT.StepSizeAdaptor).\n\nHMC uses gradients of the target measure's density, so your BATContext needs to include an ADSelector to specify which automatic differentiation backend should be used.\n\nNote: The fields of HamiltonianMC are still subject to change, and not\n\nyet part of stable public BAT API!*\n\nConstructors:\n\nHamiltonianMC(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ntarget_acceptance::Real: Default: 0.8\ntarget_acceptance_int::Tuple{Vararg{Real}}: Default: (0.9target_acceptance, one(Float64))\nstep_size::Real: Leapfrog step size, NaN selects an automatic initial step size. Default: NaN\nstep_jitter::Real: Relative random variation of the step size per transition. Default: 0.0\nmax_depth::Int64: Maximum NUTS trajectory tree depth. Default: 10\nmax_delta_energy::Real: Energy error above which a trajectory is considered divergent. Default: 1000.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.HamiltonianMC"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.IdentityTransformAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct IdentityTransformAlgorithm <: TransformAlgorithm\n\nA no-op density transform algorithm that leaves any density unchanged.\n\nConstructors:\n\nIdentityTransformAlgorithm()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.IdentityTransformAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCGlobalProposal","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MCMCGlobalProposal <: MCMCProposal\n\nMCMC proposal algorithm for drawing samples from a global proposal distribution - independent from the current position of the MCMC walker. This is an independence Metropolis-Hastings sampler, see L. Tierney, \"Markov Chains for Exploring Posterior Distributions\" (1994).\n\nIf no distribution is passed by the user, the target is checked for the best known approximation for the posterior, e.g. the prior.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMCMCGlobalProposal(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ntarget_acceptance::Real: Default: 1.0\ntarget_acceptance_int::Tuple{Vararg{Real}}: Default: (0.01, 1.0)\nglobal_proposal::Union{Nothing, MeasureBase.AbstractMeasure, Distributions.ContinuousDistribution{<:Union{Distributions.Univariate, Distributions.Multivariate}}}: Default: nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCGlobalProposal"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.IIDSampling","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct IIDSampling <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nSample via Random.rand.\n\nConstructors:\n\nIIDSampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnsamples::Int64: Default: 10 ^ 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.IIDSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.InitFromIID","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct InitFromIID <: InitvalAlgorithm\n\nGenerates initial values for sampling, optimization, etc. by random resampling from a given set of samples.\n\nConstructors:\n\nInitFromIID()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.InitFromIID"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.InitFromSamples","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct InitFromSamples <: InitvalAlgorithm\n\nGenerates initial values for sampling, optimization, etc. by direct sampling from a given i.i.d. sampleable source.\n\nConstructors:\n\nInitFromSamples()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.InitFromSamples"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.InitFromTarget","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct InitFromTarget <: InitvalAlgorithm\n\nGenerates initial values for sampling, optimization, etc. by direct i.i.d. sampling a suitable component of that target density (e.g. it's prior) that supports it.\n\nIf the target supports direct i.i.d. sampling, e.g. because it is a distribution, initial values are sampled directly from the target.\nIf the target is a posterior density, initial values are sampled from the prior (or the prior's prior if the prior is a posterior itself, etc.).\nIf the target is a sampled density, initial values are (re-)sampled from the available samples.\n\nConstructors:\n\nInitFromTarget()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.InitFromTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.InitvalAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BAT.InitvalAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for BAT initial/starting value generation algorithms.\n\nMany algorithms in BAT, like MCMC and optimization, need initial/starting values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.InitvalAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.IntegrationAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type IntegrationAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for integration algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.IntegrationAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.KishESS","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct KishESS <: EffSampleSizeAlgorithm\n\nKish's effective sample size estimator, uses only the sample weights.\n\nSee L. Kish, \"Survey Sampling\", John Wiley & Sons (1965), and effective sample size of weighted samples.\n\nConstructors:\n\nKishESS()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.KishESS"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MALAProposal","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MALAProposal <: MCMCProposal\n\nMetropolis adjusted Langevin sampling algorithm.\n\nSee G. O. Roberts and R. L. Tweedie, \"Exponential convergence of Langevin distributions and their discrete approximations\" (1996). The default target acceptance rate and the dimension-dependent step scaling follow G. O. Roberts and J. S. Rosenthal, \"Optimal scaling of discrete approximations to Langevin diffusions\" (1998); that optimality theory assumes Gaussian innovations, so with a non-Gaussian proposaldist consider setting target_acceptance explicitly.\n\nInvalid acceptance controls or a non-finite/non-positive τ_base are rejected with ArgumentError when the MCMC state is constructed.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMALAProposal(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ntarget_acceptance::Real: Target acceptance probability, strictly between zero and one. Default: 0.574\ntarget_acceptance_int::Tuple{Vararg{Real}}: Two-element ordered acceptable tuning interval within [0, 1]. Default: (0.5, 0.65)\nproposaldist::Union{MeasureBase.AbstractMeasure, Distributions.ContinuousDistribution{<:Union{Distributions.Univariate, Distributions.Multivariate}}}: Default: Normal()\nτ_base::Real: Positive finite base Langevin step scale. Default: 1.65 ^ 2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MALAProposal"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MaxDensitySearch","page":"API Documentation","text":"MaxDensitySearch <: AbstractModeEstimator\n\nConstructors:\n\nMaxDensitySearch()\n\nEstimate the mode as the variate with the highest posterior density value within a given set of samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MaxDensitySearch"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type MCMCAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for Markov chain Monte Carlo algorithms.\n\nTo implement a new MCMC algorithm, subtypes of both MCMCAlgorithm and MCMCChainState are required.\n\nnote: Note\nThe details of the MCMCIterator and MCMCAlgorithm API required to implement a new MCMC algorithm currently do not (yet) form part of the stable API and are subject to change without deprecation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCBurninAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type MCMCBurninAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for MCMC burn-in algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCBurninAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCChainPoolInit","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MCMCChainPoolInit <: MCMCInitAlgorithm\n\nMCMC chain pool initialization strategy.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMCMCChainPoolInit(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ninit_tries_per_chain::IntervalSets.ClosedInterval{Int64}: Default: ClosedInterval(8, 128)\nnsteps_init::Int64: Default: 1000\ninitval_alg::InitvalAlgorithm: Default: InitFromTarget()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCChainPoolInit"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCRetryInit","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MCMCRetryInit <: MCMCInitAlgorithm\n\nTODO\n\nConstructors:\n\nMCMCRetryInit(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nmax_init_tries::Int64: Default: 20\nnsteps_init::Int64: Default: 250\ninitval_alg::InitvalAlgorithm: Default: InitFromTarget()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCRetryInit"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCInitAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type MCMCInitAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for MCMC initialization algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCInitAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCMultiCycleBurnin","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MCMCMultiCycleBurnin <: MCMCBurninAlgorithm\n\nA multi-cycle MCMC burn-in algorithm.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMCMCMultiCycleBurnin(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnsteps_per_cycle::Int64: Default: 10000\nmax_ncycles::Int64: Default: 30\nnsteps_final::Int64: Default: div(nstepspercycle, 10)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCMultiCycleBurnin"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCMultiProposal","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MCMCMultiProposal<: MCMCProposal\n\nMCMC sampling algorithm that allows for using multiple different proposal algorithms during sampling.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMCMCMultiProposal(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nproposals::Tuple{Vararg{BAT.MCMCProposal}}\npicking_rule::Union{Vector{<:Integer}, Distributions.Categorical{P} where P<:Real}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCMultiProposal"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCProposalTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type MCMCProposalTuning\n\nAbstract type for MCMC tuning algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCProposalTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MCMCTransformTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type MCMCTransformTuning\n\nAbstract type for MCMC tuning algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCTransformTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MGVISampling","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MGVISampling <: AbstractUltraNestAlgorithmReactiv\n\nSamples via Metric Gaussian Variational Inference, using the MGVI.jl Julia implementation of the algorithm.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMGVISampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Pre-transformation to apply to the target measure before sampling.\nnsamples::Int: Number is independent samples to draw. MGVI will generate symmetical samples, so it will generate 2*nsamplessamples in total, but onlynsamples` independent samples.\nschedule::MGVISchedule: MGVI schedule, by default a FixedMGVISchedule.\nconfig::MGVI.MGVIConfig: MGVI configuration.\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the package MGVI is loaded (e.g. via import MGVI).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MGVISampling"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.ModeAsDefined","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct ModeAsDefined <: AbstractModeEstimator\n\nGet the mode as defined by the density, resp. the underlying distribution (if available), via StatsBase.mode.\n\nConstructors:\n\nModeAsDefined()        \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ModeAsDefined"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MultiProposalTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MultiProposalTuning <: MCMCProposalTuning\n\nTuning algorithm for MCMCMultiProposals.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMultiProposalTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nproposal_tunings::Tuple{Vararg{MCMCProposalTuning}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MultiProposalTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MultiTrafoTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct MultiTrafoTuning <: MCMCTransformTuning\n\nTuning algorithm for chains of adaptive transformations (see AdaptiveTransformChain): one transform tuning per chain component, each tuning its component against the samples in that component's input/output spaces.\n\nScore-based tunings (like FisherTransformTuning) are not supported as components yet, their score transport would require the chain rule through the other components.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMultiTrafoTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ntrafo_tunings::Tuple{Vararg{MCMCTransformTuning}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MultiTrafoTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.NoMCMCProposalTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"NoMCMCProposalTuning <: MCMCProposalTuning\n\nDo not perform any MCMC proposal tuning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.NoMCMCProposalTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.NoMCMCTransformTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"NoMCMCTransformTuning <: MCMCTransformTuning\n\nDo not perform any MCMC transform tuning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.NoMCMCTransformTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.OptimAlg","page":"API Documentation","text":"OptimAlg\n\nSelects an optimization algorithm from the Optim.jl package as the backend for density maximization.\n\nUsed via TransformedMaxDensity for mode estimation; a bare OptimAlg used as a mode estimator is auto-wrapped in a TransformedMaxDensity with default settings.\n\nNote that when using first order algorithms like Optim.LBFGS, your BATContext needs to include an ADSelector that specifies which automatic differentiation backend should be used.\n\nConstructors:\n\nOptimAlg(; fields...)\n\noptimalg must be an Optim.AbstractOptimizer.\n\nFields:\n\noptalg::Any: Default: extdefault(pkgext(Val(:Optim)), Val(:DEFAULTOPTALG))\nmaxiters::Int64: Default: 1000\nmaxtime::Float64: Default: NaN\nabstol::Float64: Default: NaN\nreltol::Float64: Default: 0.0\nstore_trace::Bool: Default: false\nkwargs::NamedTuple: Default: (;)\n\nnote: Note\nThis algorithm is only available if the Optim package is loaded (e.g. via     import Optim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.OptimAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.OptimizationAlg","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct OptimizationAlg\n\nSelects an optimization algorithm from the OptimizationBase.jl package as the backend for density maximization.\n\nUsed via TransformedMaxDensity for mode estimation; a bare OptimizationAlg used as a mode estimator is auto-wrapped in a TransformedMaxDensity with default settings.\n\nNote that when using first order algorithms like OptimizationOptimJL.LBFGS, your BATContext needs to have ad set to an automatic differentiation backend.\n\nConstructors:\n\nOptimizationAlg(; fields...)\n\noptalg must be an OptimizationBase.AbstractOptimizer. The field kwargs can be used to pass additional keywords to the optimizers See the OptimizationBase.jl documentation for the available keyword arguments. Fields:\n\noptalg::Any: Default: extdefault(pkgext(Val(:OptimizationBase)), Val(:DEFAULTOPTALG))\nmaxiters::Int64: Default: 1000\nmaxtime::Float64: Default: NaN\nabstol::Float64: Default: NaN\nreltol::Float64: Default: 0.0\nstore_trace::Bool: Default: false\nkwargs::NamedTuple: Default: (;)\n\nnote: Note\nThis algorithm is only available if the OptimizationBase package or any of its submodules, like OptimizationOptimJL, is loaded (e.g. via     import OptimizationOptimJL).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.OptimizationAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.PosteriorMeasure","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct PosteriorMeasure{Li,Pr<:AbstractMeasure} <: AbstractPosteriorMeasure\n\nA representation of a PosteriorMeasure, based a likelihood and prior. Likelihood and prior be accessed via\n\ngetlikelihood(posterior::PosteriorMeasure)::Li\ngetprior(posterior::PosteriorMeasure)::Pr\n\nConstructors:\n\nPosteriorMeasure(likelihood, prior)\nPosteriorMeasure{T<:Real}(likelihood, prior)\n\nFields:\n\nlikelihood::Any\nprior::MeasureBase.AbstractMeasure\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PosteriorMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.PriorSubstitution","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct PriorSubstitution <: TransformAlgorithm\n\nSubstitute the prior by a given distribution and transform the likelihood accordingly. The log(abs(jacobian)) of the transformation does not need to be auto-differentiable even for operations that use the gradient of the posterior.\n\nConstructors:\n\nPriorSubstitution()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PriorSubstitution"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.NormalBased","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct NormalBased <: TransformIntent\n\nSpecifies that the target measure of an operation should be transformed so that it is based on a standard multivariate normal distribution: the prior — descending through nested posteriors to the innermost prior — becomes standard normal. Applies to any measure with such a transformable base, not just posteriors.\n\nConstructors:\n\nNormalBased()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.NormalBased"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.UniformBased","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct UniformBased <: TransformIntent\n\nSpecifies that the target measure of an operation should be transformed so that it is based on a uniform distribution over the unit hypercube: the prior — descending through nested posteriors to the innermost prior — becomes standard uniform. Applies to any measure with such a transformable base, not just posteriors.\n\nConstructors:\n\nUniformBased()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.UniformBased"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.RAMTuning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct RAMTuning <: MCMCTransformTuning\n\nTunes MCMC spaces transformations based on M. Vihola, \"Robust adaptive Metropolis algorithm with coerced acceptance rate\" (2012).\n\nIn constrast to the original RAM algorithm, RAMTuning does not use the covariance estimate to change a proposal distribution, but instead uses it as the bases for an affine transformation. The sampling process is mathematically equivalent, though.\n\nConstructors:\n\nRAMTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ngamma::Float64: Negative adaption rate exponent. Default: 2 / 3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.RAMTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.RandomWalk","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct RandomWalk <: MCMCProposal\n\nMetropolis-Hastings MCMC sampling algorithm.\n\nFor Gaussian random-walk Metropolis in the high-dimensional symmetric product-target regime, the asymptotic target acceptance rate and proposal scaling are G. O. Roberts, A. Gelman and W. R. Gilks, \"Weak convergence and optimal scaling of random walk Metropolis algorithms\" (1997).\n\nBAT applies the same scale heuristically to its default Cauchy (TDist(1)) innovation.\n\nConstructors:\n\nRandomWalk(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ntarget_acceptance::Real: Default: 0.234\ntarget_acceptance_int::Tuple{Vararg{Real}}: Default: (0.15, 0.35)\nproposaldist::Union{MeasureBase.AbstractMeasure, Distributions.ContinuousDistribution{<:Union{Distributions.Univariate, Distributions.Multivariate}}}: Default: TDist(1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.RandomWalk"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.RandResampling","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct RandResampling <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nResamples from a given set of samples.\n\nConstructors:\n\nRandResampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnsamples::Int64: Default: 10 ^ 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.RandResampling"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.RepetitionWeighting","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct RepetitionWeighting{T<:AbstractFloat} <: AbstractMCMCWeightingScheme{T}\n\nSample weighting scheme suitable for sampling algorithms which may repeated samples multiple times in direct succession (e.g. RandomWalk). The repeated sample is stored only once, with a weight equal to the number of times it has been repeated (e.g. because a Markov chain has not moved during a sampling step).\n\nConstructors:\n\nRepetitionWeighting()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.RepetitionWeighting"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.RiceBinning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct RiceBinning <: BinningAlgorithm\n\nSelects automatic binning based on the Rice rule.\n\nConstructor: RiceBinning()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.RiceBinning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.SampleMedianEstimator","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct SampleMedianEstimator <: AbstractMedianEstimator\n\nGet median values from samples using standard Julia statistics functions.\n\nConstructors:\n\nSampleMedianEstimator()        \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SampleMedianEstimator"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.ScottBinning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct ScottBinning <: BinningAlgorithm\n\nSelects automatic binning based on Scott's normal reference rule.\n\nSee D. W. Scott, \"On optimal and data-based histograms\" (1979).\n\nConstructor: ScottBinning()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ScottBinning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.SokalAutocorLen","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct SokalAutocorLen <: AutocorLenAlgorithm\n\nIntegrated autocorrelation length estimation based on the automated windowing procedure descibed in A. D. Sokal, \"Monte Carlo Methods in Statistical Mechanics\" (1996)\n\nSame procedure is used by the emcee Python package (v3.0).\n\nConstructors:\n\nSokalAutocorLen(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nc::Int64: Step size for window search Default: 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SokalAutocorLen"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.SquareRootBinning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct SquareRootBinning <: BinningAlgorithm\n\nSelects automatic binning based on the Square-root choice.\n\nConstructor: SquareRootBinning()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SquareRootBinning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.SturgesBinning","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct SturgesBinning <: BinningAlgorithm\n\nSelects automatic binning based on Sturges' formula.\n\nSee H. A. Sturges, \"The Choice of a Class Interval\" (1926).\n\nConstructor: SturgesBinning()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SturgesBinning"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.SuaveIntegration","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct SuaveIntegration <: IntegrationAlgorithm\n\nSuaveIntegration integration algorithm.\n\nSee T. Hahn, \"Cuba - a library for multidimensional numerical integration\" (2005).\n\nConstructors:\n\nSuaveIntegration(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: UniformBased()\nrtol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:RTOL))\natol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:ATOL))\nminevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MINEVALS))\nmaxevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MAXEVALS))\nnnew::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NNEW))\nnmin::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NMIN))\nflatness::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:FLATNESS))\nnthreads::Int64: Default: Base.Threads.nthreads()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the Cuba package is loaded (e.g. via import CUBA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SuaveIntegration"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.SystematicResampling","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct SystematicResampling <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nSystematic resampling from a given series of samples, keeping the order of the samples: a single stratified uniform yields exactly nsamples draws in one order-preserving pass. It typically gives lower variance than multinomial resampling, though its conditional variance is ordering-dependent and does not uniformly dominate the other standard resampling schemes.\n\nSee G. Kitagawa, \"Monte Carlo Filter and Smoother for Non-Gaussian Nonlinear State Space Models\", J. Comput. Graph. Stat. 5(1) (1996).\n\nCan be used to efficiently convert weighted samples into samples with unity weights.\n\nConstructors:\n\nSystematicResampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnsamples::Int64: Default: 10 ^ 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SystematicResampling"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.ToRealVector","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct ToRealVector <: TransformIntent\n\nSpecifies that the input should be transformed into a measure over the space of real-valued flat vectors.\n\nConstructors:\n\nToRealVector()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ToRealVector"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.TransformAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type TransformAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for density transformation algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.TransformAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.TransformedMaxDensity","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct TransformedMaxDensity <: AbstractModeEstimator\n\nEstimates the mode of a measure by maximizing its density numerically, searching in a transformed space.\n\nThe search runs in the space induced by pretransform without applying the transformation's volume correction, so the result is a mode of the original density, not of the transformed one.\n\nConstructors:\n\nTransformedMaxDensity(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\noptalg::Union{OptimAlg, OptimizationAlg}: Density maximization backend. Default: OptimAlg()\npretransform::TransformIntent: Target space transformation to search in. Default: NormalBased()\ninit::InitvalAlgorithm: Initial point selection, applied in the transformed space. Default: InitFromTarget()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.TransformedMaxDensity"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.TransformedMCMC","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct TransformedMCMC <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nSamples a probability density using Markov chain Monte Carlo.\n\nCancellation of MCMC chain groups is cooperative. On a sampling-owner exception, BAT requests cancellation, releases observation waits and joins all launched chain workers before propagating the exception. An already-entered numerical step or user hook must return before its worker can stop; arbitrary native code cannot be forcibly interrupted. Observation timeouts do not impose a numerical worker shutdown deadline.\n\nConstructors:\n\nTransformedMCMC(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nproposal::BAT.MCMCProposal: Default: RandomWalk(proposaldist = TDist(1.0))\nproposal_tuning::MCMCProposalTuning: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:proposaltuning), proposal)\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: bat_default(TransformedMCMC, Val(:pretransform), proposal)\nadaptive_transform::BAT.AbstractAdaptiveTransform: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:adaptivetransform), proposal)\ntransform_tuning::MCMCTransformTuning: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:transformtuning), proposal, adaptive_transform)\ntempering::MCMCTempering: Default: bat_default(TransformedMCMC, Val(:tempering), proposal)\nnchains::Int64: Default: 4\nnwalkers::Int64: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:nwalkers), proposal, pretransform, transformtuning, nchains)\nnsteps::Int64: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:nsteps), proposal, pretransform, transformtuning, nchains, nwalkers)\ninit::MCMCInitAlgorithm: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:init), proposal, pretransform, transformtuning, nchains, nwalkers, nsteps)\nburnin::MCMCBurninAlgorithm: Default: batdefault(TransformedMCMC, Val(:burnin), proposal, pretransform, transformtuning, nchains, nwalkers, nsteps)\nconvergence::BAT.ConvergenceTest: Default: BrooksGelmanConvergence()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\nstore_burnin::Bool: Default: false\nnonzero_weights::Bool: Default: true\nsample_weighting::AbstractMCMCWeightingScheme: Default: RepetitionWeighting()\ncallback::Function: With MCMCMultiCycleBurnin and store_burnin = true, called as callback(Val(:mcmc_burnin), mcmc_states) after each tuning cycle, with the vector of chain states. The return value is ignored. No user callback is invoked during initialization, post-tuning stabilization or main sampling.  Default: nop_func\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.TransformedMCMC"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.VEGASIntegration","page":"API Documentation","text":"struct VEGASIntegration <: IntegrationAlgorithm\n\nVEGASIntegration integration algorithm.\n\nSee T. Hahn, \"Cuba - a library for multidimensional numerical integration\" (2005).\n\nConstructors:\n\nVEGASIntegration(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: UniformBased()\nrtol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:RTOL))\natol::Float64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:ATOL))\nminevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MINEVALS))\nmaxevals::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:MAXEVALS))\nnstart::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NSTART))\nnincrease::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NINCREASE))\nnbatch::Int64: Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:Cuba)), Val(:NBATCH))\nnthreads::Int64: Default: Base.Threads.nthreads()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the Cuba package is loaded (e.g. via import CUBA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.VEGASIntegration"},{"category":"function","location":"stable_api/#BAT.unevaluated","page":"API Documentation","text":"BAT.unevaluated(obj)\n\nIf obj is an evaluated object, like a EvaluatedMeasure, return the original (unevaluated) object. Otherwise, return obj.\n\nThis is the explicit way to strip attached measure knowledge, e.g. to obtain a bare measure for performance-critical density evaluation. Reparametrizations like unshaped transport attached knowledge instead of dropping it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.unevaluated"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AbstractMedianEstimator","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BAT.AbstractMedianEstimator\n\nAbstract type for BAT median estimation algorithms (see bat_findmedian).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractMedianEstimator"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AbstractModeEstimator","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BAT.AbstractModeEstimator\n\nAbstract type for BAT optimization algorithms.\n\nA typical application for optimization in BAT is mode estimation (see bat_findmode),\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractModeEstimator"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.AbstractSamplingAlgorithm","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BAT.AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nAbstract type for BAT sampling algorithms. See bat_sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractSamplingAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.ConvergenceTest","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type ConvergenceTest\n\nAbstract type for integrated autocorrelation length estimation algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ConvergenceTest"},{"category":"type","location":"stable_api/#BAT.MGVISchedule","page":"API Documentation","text":"abstract type BAT.MGVISchedule\n\nAbstract supertype for MGVI sampling schedules.\n\nSee MGVISampling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MGVISchedule"},{"category":"section","location":"faq/#Frequently-Asked-Questions","page":"Frequently Asked Questions","text":"... to be written ...","title":"Frequently Asked Questions"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Developer-Instructions","page":"Developer instructions","text":"","title":"Developer Instructions"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Documentation-Generation","page":"Developer instructions","text":"To generate and view a local version of the documentation, run\n\ncd docs\njulia make.jl\n\nthen open \"docs/build/index.html\" in your browser.","title":"Documentation Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Code-Reloading","page":"Developer instructions","text":"When changing the code of BAT.jl and testing snippets and examples in the REPL, automatic code reloading comes in very handy. Try out Revise.jl.","title":"Code Reloading"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#List-of-BAT-algorithms","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT offers multiple algorithms for sampling, integration and optimization:","title":"List of BAT algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Sampling-algorithms","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT function: bat_sample","title":"Sampling algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#IIDSampling","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: IIDSampling\n\nbat_sample(target.prior, IIDSampling(nsamples=10^5))","title":"IIDSampling"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Metropolis-Hastings","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: TransformedMCMC, MCMC algorithm subtype: RandomWalk\n\nbat_sample(target, TransformedMCMC(proposal = RandomWalk(), nsteps = 10^5, nchains = 4))","title":"Metropolis-Hastings"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Hamiltonian-MC","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: TransformedMCMC, MCMC algorithm subtype: HamiltonianMC\n\nimport ForwardDiff\nset_batcontext(ad = ForwardDiff)\nbat_sample(target, TransformedMCMC(proposal = HamiltonianMC()))","title":"Hamiltonian MC"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Reactive-Nested-Sampling-(experimental)","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: ReactiveNestedSampling\n\nimport UltraNest\nbat_sample(target, ReactiveNestedSampling())\n\nRequires the UltraNest Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Reactive Nested Sampling (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Ellipsoidal-Nested-Sampling-(experimental)","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: EllipsoidalNestedSampling\n\nimport NestedSamplers\nbat_sample(target, EllipsoidalNestedSampling())\n\nRequires the NestedSamplers Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Ellipsoidal Nested Sampling (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Elliptical-slice-sampling-(experimental)","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: EllipticalSliceMCMCSampling\n\nimport EllipticalSliceSampling\nbat_sample(posterior, EllipticalSliceMCMCSampling(nsamples = 10^4))\n\nBAT maps the prior to a standard Gaussian before sampling. Requires the EllipticalSliceSampling Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Elliptical slice sampling (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Slice-sampling-(experimental)","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: SliceMCMCSampling\n\nimport SliceSampling\nbat_sample(target, SliceMCMCSampling(nsamples = 10^4))\n\nPass any SliceSampling.jl sampler through the sampler field. Requires the SliceSampling Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Slice sampling (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Sobol-Sampler","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: SobolSampler\n\nbat_sample(target, SobolSampler(nsamples=10^5))","title":"Sobol Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Grid-Sampler","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: GridSampler\n\nbat_sample(target, GridSampler(ppa=100))","title":"Grid Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Prior-Importance-Sampler","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT sampling algorithm type: PriorImportanceSampler\n\nbat_sample(target, PriorImportanceSampler(nsamples=10^5))","title":"Prior Importance Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Integration-algorithms","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT function: bat_integrate","title":"Integration algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Vegas-Integration","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT integration algorithm type: VEGASIntegration\n\nimport Cuba\nbat_integrate(target, VEGASIntegration())\n\nRequires the Cuba Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Vegas Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Suave-Integration","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT integration algorithm type: SuaveIntegration\n\nimport Cuba\nbat_integrate(target, SuaveIntegration())\n\nRequires the Cuba Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Suave Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Cuhre-Integration","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT integration algorithm type: CuhreIntegration\n\nimport Cuba\nbat_integrate(target, CuhreIntegration())\n\nRequires the Cuba Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Cuhre Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Divonne-Integration","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT integration algorithm type: DivonneIntegration \n\nimport Cuba\nbat_integrate(target, DivonneIntegration())\n\nRequires the Cuba Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Divonne Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Integration-via-Bridge-Sampling-(experimental)","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT integration algorithm type: BridgeSampling \n\nbat_integrate(EvaluatedMeasure(target, empirical = smpls), BridgeSampling())","title":"Integration via Bridge Sampling (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Mode-finding-algorithms","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT function: bat_findmode","title":"Mode finding algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Optim.jl-Optimization-Algorithms","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT mode finding algorithm type: TransformedMaxDensity with an OptimAlg backend.\n\nusing Optim\nbat_findmode(target, TransformedMaxDensity(optalg = OptimAlg(optalg = Optim.NelderMead())))\n\nimport ForwardDiff\nset_batcontext(ad = ForwardDiff)\nbat_findmode(target, TransformedMaxDensity(optalg = OptimAlg(optalg = Optim.LBFGS())))\n\nBare backend configurations and raw Optim.jl optimizers are auto-wrapped in a TransformedMaxDensity with default settings, so bat_findmode(target, Optim.NelderMead()) works as well.\n\nRequires the Optim Julia package to be loaded explicitly.","title":"Optim.jl Optimization Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#OptimizationBase.jl-Optimization-Algorithms","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT mode finding algorithm type: TransformedMaxDensity with an OptimizationAlg backend.\n\nusing OptimizationOptimJL\n\nalg = TransformedMaxDensity(optalg = OptimizationAlg(;\n    optalg = OptimizationOptimJL.ParticleSwarm(n_particles=10),\n    maxiters=200,\n    kwargs=(f_calls_limit=50,)\n))\nbat_findmode(target, alg)\n\nRequires the desired package that implements the OptimizationBase.jl interface to be loaded (e.g. via import OptimizationOptimJL).","title":"OptimizationBase.jl Optimization Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Maximum-Sample-Estimator","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT mode finding algorithm type: MaxDensitySearch \n\nbat_findmode(smpls, MaxDensitySearch())","title":"Maximum Sample Estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#File-I/O","page":"List of algorithms","text":"","title":"File-I/O"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#Plain-HDF5","page":"List of algorithms","text":"BAT I/O algorithm type: BATHDF5IO \n\nimport HDF5\nbat_write(\"results.h5\", smpls)\n\n# ... later ...\n\nsmpls = bat_read(\"results.h5\").result","title":"Plain HDF5"},{"category":"section","location":"list_of_algorithms/#JLD2","page":"List of algorithms","text":"Not BAT-specific, JLD2 is able to handle complex Julia data structures in general.\n\nusing FileIO\nimport JLD2\nFileIO.save(\"results.jld2\", Dict(\"smpls\" => smpls))\n\n# ... later ...\n\nsmpls = FileIO.load(\"results.jld2\", \"smpls\")","title":"JLD2"},{"category":"section","location":"license/#License","page":"License","text":"The BAT.jl package is licensed under the MIT \"Expat\" License:\n\nCopyright (c) 2017-2021:Oliver Schulz <oschulz@mpp.mpg.de>    and contributorsPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.\n\n","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plot-Recipes","page":"Plotting","text":"The plot recipes described below apply for plotting samples. Only the plotting of estimators (mean, standard deviation, global mode and local mode) is currently only supported for samples.\n\nMarginalDist constructed from samples checks both histogram probabilities and the binned distribution's cached coordinate statistics. These statistics use bin centers, so a single occupied bin has zero variance in exact arithmetic; no within-bin variance term is added. Construction throws ArgumentError if coordinate arithmetic overflows, loses a nonzero result, produces a subnormal intermediate/statistic, or disagrees with the checked dependency result. This conservative check can reject a histogram even when wider arithmetic could represent its moments. Ordinary floating-point rounding and cancellation still apply. Gradual underflow must be enabled, and BigFloat arithmetic requires round-to-nearest; construction does not change either setting. Existing distribution wrappers and direct EmpiricalDistributions constructors do not pass through this sample-construction check. Bin edges use the floating coordinate precision of the cached statistics; edges that collapse under conversion are rejected.\n\nAlso see the plotting tutorial for examples and further information on the plotting options.","title":"Plot Recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#1D-plots","page":"Plotting","text":"plot(\n    samples::DensitySampleVector,\n    vsel::Union{Integer, Symbol, Expr};\n    intervals = BAT.default_credibilities,\n    bins = 200,\n    colors = BAT.default_colors,\n    interval_labels = [],\n    mean = false,\n    std = false,\n    globalmode = false,\n    marginalmode = true,\n    filter = false,\n    closed = :left\n)\n\nRequired inputs:\n\nsamples::DensitySampleVector: samples to be plotted\nvsel::Union{Integer, Symbol, Expr}: index or name of the parameter to be plotted. A name can also select an element of a multivariate parameter, :(a[2]), or a field of a nested named tuple, :(x.b.c)\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nseriestype::Symbol = :smallest_intervals: plot style        \n\navailable seriestypes:\n* `:smallest_intervals` (default for samples), alias `:HDR`\n* `:central_intervals`\n* `:histogram`, alias `:steppost`\n* `:stephist`\n\nbins::Union{Integer, AbstractRange} = 200: number of histogram bins or bin edges.\nintervals::Array{<:Real, 1} = [0.683, 0.955, 0.997]: probability to be enclosed in the smallest/central intervals when using the corresponding seriestypes\ncolors::Array{Symbol, 1} = [:chartreuse2, :yellow, :red]: colors of the smallest/central interval regions, in same order as the values in intervals\nmean::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate mean value (currently only for samples), calculated via bat_stats().mean.\n\nThe plot style of the mean can be customized using a Dict.  For mean = true, the default style is:   Dict(\"linestyle\" => :solid, \"linewidth\" => 1, \"linecolor\" => :dimgrey, \"alpha\" => 1, \"label\" => \"mean\")\n\nstd::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate the standard deviation of the mean (currently only for samples), calculated from bat_stats().cov. The style of the standard deviation can be customized using a Dict.  For std = true, the default style is:   \n\nDict(\"fillcolor\" => :grey, \"fillalpha\" => 0.5, \"label\" => \"std. dev.\")\n\nglobalmode::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate global mode (currently only for samples), calculated via bat_stats().mode.  The style of the global mode can be passed as a Dict.  For globalmode = true, the default style is:  \n\nDict(\"linestyle\" => :dash, \"linewidth\" => 1, \"linecolor\" => :black, \"alpha\" => 1, \"label\" => \"global mode\")\n\nmarginalmode::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate the marginal mode(s), i.e. the center of the highest histogram bin(s) (currently only for samples). The style can be passed as a Dict. If marginalmode = true, the default style is:  \n\nDict(\"linestyle\" => :dot, \"linewidth\" => 1, \"linecolor\" => :black, \"alpha\" => 1, \"label\" => \"marginal mode\")\n\n(only for samples) filter::Bool = false: if true, BAT.drop_low_weight_samples() is applied before plotting\nclosed = :left: see StatsBase.Histogram\n\nKeyword arguments for attributes supported by Plots.jl can also be passed to the function.","title":"1D plots"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#2D-plots","page":"Plotting","text":"plot(\n    samples::DensitySampleVector,\n    vsel::Union{NTuple{2, Integer}, NTuple{2, Union{Symbol, Expr}}};\n    intervals = BAT.default_credibilities,\n\tinterval_labels = [],\n    colors = BAT.default_colors,\n\tbins = 200,\n    mean = false,\n    std = false,\n    globalmode = false,\n    marginalmode = true,\n\tsmoothing = 0,\n    diagonal = Dict(),\n    upper = Dict(),\n    right = Dict(),\n    filter = false,\n    closed = :left\n)\n\nRequired inputs:\n\nsamples::DensitySampleVector: samples to be plotted\nvsel::Union{NTuple{2, Integer}, NTuple{2, Union{Symbol, Expr}}}: indices or names of the two parameters to be plotted\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nseriestype::Symbol = :smallest_intervals: plot style  \n\navailable seriestypes:\n* `:smallest_intervals` (default for samples)\n* `:smallest_intervals_contour`\n* `:smallest_intervals_contourf`: filled contours)\n* `:histogram`, alias `:hist`, alias `:histogram2d`\n* `:scatter`\n\nbins::Union{Integer, NTuple{2, Integer}, NTuple{2, AbstractRange}} = 200: number of histogram bins or bin edges. Use a NTuple{2, Union{Integer, AbstractRange}} to specify bins/edges of x and y axes separately.\nintervals::Array{<:Real, 1} = [0.683, 0.955, 0.997]  probability to be enclosed in the smallest intervals when using the corresponding seriestypes\ninterval_labels = []  label of the intervals as strings, LatexStrings are possible.\ncolors::Array{Symbol, 1} = [:chartreuse2, :yellow, :red] colors of the smallest interval regions, in same order as the values in intervals\nmean::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate mean value (currently only for samples) , calculated via bat_stats().mean.\n\nThe plot style of the mean can be customized using a Dict.  For mean = true, the default style is: Dict(\"markercolor\" => :black, \"markersize\" => 4, \"markeralpha\" => 1, \"markershape\" => :circle, \"markerstrokealpha\" => 1, \"markerstrokecolor\" => :black, \"markerstrokestyle\" => :solid, \"markerstrokewidth\" => 1, \"label\" => \"mean\")\n\nstd::Union{Bool} = true: indicate the standard deviation of the mean as errorbars (currently only for samples), calculated from bat_stats().cov. The style of the errorbars can be customized using the markerstroke... options in mean.\nglobalmode::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate global mode (currently only for samples), calculated via bat_stats().mode.  The style of the global mode can be passed as a Dict.  For globalmode = true, the default style is:\n\nDict(\"linestyle\" => :dash, \"linewidth\" => 1, \"linecolor\" => :black, \"alpha\" => 1, \"label\" => \"global mode\")\n\nmarginalmode::Union{Dict, Bool} = true: indicate the marginalmode(s), i.e. the center of the highest histogram bin(s) (currently only for samples). The style can be passed as a Dict. If marginalmode = true, the default style is:\n\nDict(\"linestyle\" => :dot, \"linewidth\" => 1, \"linecolor\" => :black, \"alpha\" => 1, \"label\" => \"marginal mode\")\n\nsmoothing = 0: Gaussian smoothing for contours, specified by a finite, nonnegative real standard deviation in bin-index units. A scalar applies to both axes; a tuple such as (0.5, 1.0) specifies each axis separately. Zero disables smoothing on that axis, so (0, 1) smooths only the second axis. Each axis uses radius 2*ceil(sigma), with zero padding and cropping to the displayed grid. Requests exceeding the internal kernel, numeric-buffer or convolution-work budgets raise an ArgumentError; reduce smoothing or the number of bins, or use whole-bin regions. See the contour contract for resource limits and probability semantics.\ndiagonal = Dict(): Used only for the seriestype :marginal. The dictionary can contain the seriestypes and plot options for 2D distributions explained above to modify the 2D plot of the marginal plot. Nested Dictionaries are possible to modify the styles of the estimators as described above\nupper = Dict() and right = Dict():  Used only for the seriestype :marginal. The dictionaries can contain the seriestypes and plot options for 1D distributions explained above to modify the upper and right plots of the 1D marginal distributions. Nested Dictionaries are possible to modify the styles of the estimators as described above\nfilter::Bool = false: if true, BAT.drop_low_weight_samples() is applied before plotting\nclosed = :left: see StatsBase.Histogram\n\nKeyword arguments for attributes supported by Plots.jl can also be passed to the function.","title":"2D plots"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#1D-and-2D-overview-plots","page":"Plotting","text":"plot(\n\tsamples::DensitySampleVector;\n    vsel=collect(1:5),\n\tbins = 200,\n    mean=false,\n    std=false,\n    globalmode=false,\n    marginalmode=false,\n    diagonal = Dict(),\n    upper = Dict(),\n    lower = Dict(),\n    vsel_label = []\n)\n\nPlot a grid with the 1D marginalized distributions on the diagonal and all combinations of the 2D marginalized distributions in the lower and upper triangle.\n\nRequired inputs:\n\nsamples::DensitySampleVector: samples to be plotted\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nvsel = collect(1:5): indices or parameter names of the parameters to be plotted. By default (up to) the first five parameters are plotted.\nbins::Union{Integer, Tuple{Union{Integer, AbstractRange}}, NamedTuple} = 200: Number of bins or bin edges\nmean::Bool = false: Indicate mean value, calculated via  bat_stats().mean, in all plots (currently only for samples)\nstd::Bool = false: Indicate the standard deviation of the mean calculated from bat_stats().cov in all plots (currently only for samples)\nglobalmode::Bool = false: Indicate global mode, calculated via bat_stats().mode, in all plots (currently only for samples)\nmarginalmode::Bool = false: Indicate local mode(s), i.e. the center of the highest histogram bin(s), in all plots (currently only for samples)\ndiagonal = Dict(): The dictionary can contain the seriestypes and plot options for 1D distributions explained above to modify the plots of the 1D marginal distributions on the diagonal of the grid. Nested Dictionaries are possible to modify the styles of the estimators as described above\nlower = Dict() and upper = Dict():  The dictionaries can contain the seriestypes and plot options for 2D distributions explained above to modify the 2D plots in the lower and upper triangles of the grid. Nested Dictionaries are possible to modify the styles of the estimators as described above\nvsel_label = []: parameter label as strings, LatexStrings are possible","title":"1D & 2D overview plots"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"BAT.jl is written in the Julia programming language. To use BAT.jl, you will need to install Julia itself, the BAT.jl Julia package, and some additional Julia packages. Depending on your use case, you may also want to need a Python installation with certain Python packages (optional).\n\nTable of contents:\n\nPages = [\"installation.md\"]\nDepth = 3","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installing-Julia","page":"Installation","text":"Julia is available for Linux, OS-X and Windows, and easy to install:\n\nDownload Julia.\nExtract the archive (Linux), resp. drag Julia into Applications (OS-X) or run the installer (Windows).\nYou may want to add the Julia bin directory to your $PATH. To get the location of the Julia bin directory on OS-X or Windows, start a Julia session (via applications menu) and run the Julia command Sys.BINDIR.\n\nnote: Note\nBAT.jl requires Julia >= v1.10, we recommend to use the latest stable Julia version for optimal performance.","title":"Installing Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installing-BAT.jl-and-related-Julia-packages","page":"Installation","text":"BAT.jl is provided as a registered Julia package. To install it, simply run\n\njulia> using Pkg\njulia> pkg\"add BAT\"\n\nHowever, you will likely need other Julia packages too. We recommend that you install certain statistics, plotting, I/O and array packages as well:\n\njulia> using Pkg\njulia> pkg\"add BAT ArraysOfArrays Distributions ElasticArrays IntervalSets Parameters Plots StatsBase Tables TypedTables\"\n\nIn addition, these packages will need to be installed and loaded (using PackageName or import PackageName) to enable some optional BAT algorithms/functionalities:\n\njulia> pkg\"add AutoDiffOperators Cuba Folds HDF5 NestedSamplers Optim\"\n\nTo install the latest development version of BAT (main branch) instead of the latest stable release, use\n\njulia> pkg\"add BAT#main\"","title":"Installing BAT.jl and related Julia packages"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installing-Visual-Studio-Code-and-Jupyter-(Optional)","page":"Installation","text":"Please download and install the lastest Julia release.\n\nYou may also want to install Visual Studio Code with the VS-Code Julia extension and/or a have a working Jupyter installation. JupyterLab Desktop is easy to install (but a full Anaconda or custom Python installation with Jupyter will work too, of course). For details regarding Julia and Jupyter, see the IJulia.jl documentation.","title":"Installing Visual Studio Code and Jupyter (Optional)"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#IJulia-(Jupyter-Julia-kernel)","page":"Installation","text":"To use the the Julia Jupyter kernel, you need to add the package \"IJulia.jl\":\n\nOn Linux, simply use\n\npkg\"add IJulia\"\n\nOn OS-X, if you have an existing Jupyter installation (e.g. via Anaconda) and would like Julia to use it (instead of an internal Conda installation, see above), use (e.g.)\n\nENV[\"JUPYTER\"] = \"$(homedir())/opt/anaconda3/bin/jupyter\"; pkg\"add IJulia\"\n\nOn Windows, if would like Julia to use an existing Jupyter installation (see above), use something like\n\nENV[\"JUPYTER\"] = \"DRIVE:/path/to/your/anaconda/.../jupyter.exe\"; pkg\"add IJulia\"\n\nJulia will remember the chosen Jupyter installation permanently, ENV[\"JUPYTER\"] only needs to be set the first time you run pkg\"add IJulia\".","title":"IJulia (Jupyter Julia kernel)"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Environment-variables","page":"Installation","text":"You may want/need to set the following environment variables:\n\n$PATH: Include the Julia bin-directory in your binary search path, see above. On OS-X and Windows, Visual Studio Code should detect the path your Julia binary automatically, if installed in the default location.\n$JULIA_NUM_THREADS: Number of threads to use for Julia multi-threading\n$JULIA_DEPOT_PATH and JULIA_PKG_DEVDIR: If you want Julia to install packages in another location than $HOME/.julia.\n\nSee the Julia manual for a description of other Julia-specific environment variables.","title":"Environment variables"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Additional-customization-options","page":"Installation","text":"Note: If you want Julia to install packages in another location than $HOME/.julia, set the environment variables JULIA_DEPOT_PATH and JULIA_PKG_DEVDIR (see above).","title":"Additional customization options"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Tutorial","page":"Tutorial","text":"This tutorial demonstrates a simple application of BAT.jl: A Bayesian fit of a histogram with two Gaussian peaks.\n\nYou can also download this tutorial as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.\n\nTable of contents:\n\nPages = [\"tutorial.md\"]\nDepth = 3\n\nNote: This tutorial is somewhat verbose, as it aims to be easy to follow for users who are new to Julia. For the same reason, we deliberately avoid making use of Julia features like closures, anonymous functions, broadcasting syntax, performance annotations, etc.","title":"Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Input-Data-Generation","page":"Tutorial","text":"First, let's generate some synthetic data to fit. We'll need the Julia standard-library packages \"Random\", \"LinearAlgebra\" and \"Statistics\", as well as the packages \"Distributions\" and \"StatsBase\":\n\nusing Random, LinearAlgebra, Statistics, Distributions, StatsBase\n\nAs the underlying truth of our input data/histogram, let us choose the expected count to follow the sum of two Gaussian peaks with peak areas of 500 and 1000, a mean of -1.0 and 2.0 and a standard error of 0.5. Then\n\ndata = vcat(\n    rand(Normal(-1.0, 0.5), 500),\n    rand(Normal( 2.0, 0.5), 1000)\n)\n1500-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.5711648263144845\n -1.981403642599544\n -0.5925318446956369\n -1.3953188259439009\n -0.7310889073475816\n -1.178270263282143\n -0.41014098621468786\n -0.9807578330346706\n -0.7452646230114451\n -1.0546974287938\n  ⋮\n  1.9288780983320883\n  1.379725339336402\n  1.9715532690790782\n  1.593583537342743\n  1.3939067531044675\n  1.6736203003452321\n  1.913034928417856\n  2.2203027739289802\n  1.1962733958254157\n\nresulting in a vector of floating-point numbers:\n\ntypeof(data) == Vector{Float64}\ntrue\n\nNext, we'll create a histogram of that data, this histogram will serve as the input for the Bayesian fit:\n\nhist = append!(Histogram(-2:0.1:4), data)\nStatsBase.Histogram{Int64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}\nedges:\n  -2.0:0.1:4.0\nweights: [12, 12, 19, 17, 22, 19, 28, 35, 32, 40  …  7, 10, 2, 5, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false\n\nUsing the Julia \"Plots\" package\n\nusing Plots\n\nwe can plot the histogram:\n\nplot(\n    normalize(hist, mode=:density),\n    st = :steps, label = \"Data\",\n    title = \"Data\"\n)\nsavefig(\"tutorial-data.pdf\")\n\n(Image: Data)\n\nLet's define our fit function - the function that we expect to describe the data histogram, at each x-Axis position x, depending on a given set p of model parameters:\n\nfunction fit_function(p::NamedTuple{(:a, :mu, :sigma)}, x::Real)\n    p.a[1] * pdf(Normal(p.mu[1], p.sigma), x) +\n    p.a[2] * pdf(Normal(p.mu[2], p.sigma), x)\nend\n\nThe fit parameters (model parameters) a (peak areas) and mu (peak means) are vectors, parameter sigma (peak width) is a scalar, we assume it's the same for both Gaussian peaks.\n\nThe true values for the model/fit parameters are the values we used to generate the data:\n\ntrue_par_values = (a = [500, 1000], mu = [-1.0, 2.0], sigma = 0.5)\n\nLet's visually compare the histogram and the fit function, using these true parameter values, to make sure everything is set up correctly:\n\nplot(\n    normalize(hist, mode=:density),\n    st = :steps, label = \"Data\",\n    title = \"Data and True Statistical Model\"\n)\nplot!(\n    -4:0.01:4, x -> fit_function(true_par_values, x),\n    label = \"Truth\"\n)\nsavefig(\"tutorial-data-and-truth.pdf\")\n\n(Image: Data and True Statistical Model)","title":"Input Data Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Bayesian-Fit","page":"Tutorial","text":"Now we'll perform a Bayesian fit of the generated histogram, using BAT, to infer the model parameters from the data histogram.\n\nIn addition to the Julia packages loaded above, we need BAT itself, as well as IntervalSets:\n\nusing BAT, DensityInterface, IntervalSets","title":"Bayesian Fit"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Likelihood-Definition","page":"Tutorial","text":"First, we need to define the likelihood for our problem.\n\nBAT expects likelihoods to implements the DensityInterface API. We can simply wrap a log-likelihood function with DensityInterface.logfuncdensity to make it compatible.\n\nFor performance reasons, functions should [not access global variables directly] (https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/manual/performance-tips/index.html#Avoid-global-variables-1). So we'll use an anonymous function inside of a let-statement to capture the value of the global variable hist in a local variable h (and to shorten function name fit_function to f, purely for convenience). DensityInterface.logfuncdensity then turns the log-likelihood function into a DensityInterface density object.\n\nlikelihood = let h = hist, f = fit_function\n    # Histogram counts for each bin as an array:\n    observed_counts = h.weights\n\n    # Histogram binning:\n    bin_edges = h.edges[1]\n    bin_edges_left = bin_edges[1:end-1]\n    bin_edges_right = bin_edges[2:end]\n    bin_widths = bin_edges_right - bin_edges_left\n    bin_centers = (bin_edges_right + bin_edges_left) / 2\n\n    logfuncdensity(function (params)\n        # Log-likelihood for a single bin:\n        function bin_log_likelihood(i)\n            # Simple mid-point rule integration of fit function `f` over bin:\n            expected_counts = bin_widths[i] * f(params, bin_centers[i])\n            # Avoid zero expected counts for numerical stability:\n            logpdf(Poisson(expected_counts + eps(expected_counts)), observed_counts[i])\n        end\n\n        # Sum log-likelihood over bins:\n        idxs = eachindex(observed_counts)\n        ll_value = bin_log_likelihood(idxs[1])\n        for i in idxs[2:end]\n            ll_value += bin_log_likelihood(i)\n        end\n\n        return ll_value\n    end)\nend\nLogFuncDensity(Main.var\"#5#6\"{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}, StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}, Vector{Int64}, typeof(Main.fit_function)}(-1.95:0.1:3.95, StepRangeLen(0.1, 0.0, 60), [12, 12, 19, 17, 22, 19, 28, 35, 32, 40  …  7, 10, 2, 5, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0], Main.fit_function))\n\nBAT makes use of Julia's parallel programming facilities if possible, e.g. to run multiple Markov chains in parallel. Therefore, log-likelihood (and other) code must be thread-safe. Mark non-thread-safe code with @critical (provided by Julia package ParallelProcessingTools).\n\nSupport for automatic parallelization across multiple (local and remote) Julia processes is planned, but not implemented yet.\n\nNote that Julia currently starts only a single thread by default. Set the the environment variable JULIA_NUM_THREADS to specify the desired number of Julia threads.\n\nWe can evaluate likelihood, e.g. at the true parameter values:\n\nlogdensityof(likelihood, true_par_values)\n-155.70579003535997","title":"Likelihood Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Prior-Definition","page":"Tutorial","text":"Next, we need to choose a sensible prior for the fit:\n\nprior = distprod(\n    a = [Weibull(1.1, 5000), Weibull(1.1, 5000)],\n    mu = [-2.0..0.0, 1.0..3.0],\n    sigma = Weibull(1.2, 2)\n)\n\nBAT supports most Distributions.Distribution types, and combinations of them, as priors.","title":"Prior Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Bayesian-Model-Definition","page":"Tutorial","text":"Given the likelihood and prior definition, a BAT.PosteriorMeasure is simply defined via\n\nposterior = PosteriorMeasure(likelihood, prior)","title":"Bayesian Model Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Parameter-Space-Exploration-via-MCMC","page":"Tutorial","text":"We can now use Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) to explore the space of possible parameter values for the histogram fit.\n\nTo increase the verbosity level of BAT logging output, you may want to set the Julia logging level for BAT to debug via bat_logdebug().\n\nNow we can generate a set of MCMC samples via bat_sample. We'll use 4 MCMC chains with 10^5 MC steps in each chain (after tuning/burn-in):\n\nsamples = bat_sample(posterior, TransformedMCMC(proposal = RandomWalk(), nsteps = 10^5, nchains = 4)).result\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMCChainPoolInit: trying to generate 4 viable MCMC chain state(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mSelected 4 MCMC chain state(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mBegin tuning of 4 MCMC chain(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMC Tuning cycle 1 finished, 4 chains, 3 tuned, 0 converged.\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMC Tuning cycle 2 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 4 converged.\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMC tuning of 4 chains successful after 2 cycle(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mRunning post-tuning stabilization steps for 4 MCMC chain(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mGenerate main samples using 4 MCMC chain(s).\n\nLet's calculate some statistics on the posterior samples:\n\nprintln(\"Truth: $true_par_values\")\nprintln(\"Mode: $(mode(samples))\")\nprintln(\"Mean: $(mean(samples))\")\nprintln(\"Stddev: $(std(samples))\")\nTruth: (a = [500, 1000], mu = [-1.0, 2.0], sigma = 0.5)\nMode: (a = [499.81606210348536, 999.669293795825], mu = [-1.0259423364383016, 1.9926152133203199], sigma = 0.5104428178799523)\nMean: (a = [503.2346655210997, 999.8156051405888], mu = [-1.0260919199374952, 1.991145521794844], sigma = 0.5101639295593859)\nStddev: (a = [22.807093342036385, 31.630732377009323], mu = [0.025293162857041862, 0.01626684658950735], sigma = 0.010122064030683477)\n\nInternally, BAT often needs to represent variates as flat real-valued vectors:\n\nunshaped_samples, f_flatten = bat_transform(Vector, samples)\n(result = DensitySampleVector(length = 94065, varshape = ValueShapes.ArrayShape{Float64, 1}((5,))), f_transform = Base.Fix2{typeof(ValueShapes.unshaped), ValueShapes.NamedTupleShape{(:a, :mu, :sigma), Tuple{ValueShapes.ValueAccessor{ValueShapes.ArrayShape{Real, 1}}, ValueShapes.ValueAccessor{ValueShapes.ArrayShape{Real, 1}}, ValueShapes.ValueAccessor{ValueShapes.ScalarShape{Real}}}, NamedTuple}}(ValueShapes.unshaped, NamedTupleShape((a = ValueShapes.ArrayShape{Real, 1}((2,)), mu = ValueShapes.ArrayShape{Real, 1}((2,)), sigma = ValueShapes.ScalarShape{Real}()))), optargs = (algorithm = BAT.UnshapeTransformation(), context = BATContext{Float64}(Random123.Philox4x{UInt64, 10}(0xccaeb7bbbf07dfbc, 0xde5e77d2b95504c2, 0xf48c70eff015815d, 0x1fc9f3d5d7c49fde, 0x79947dee2719103a, 0xc80a3dc32b108cfc, 0x0000000000000000, 0x0000000000000000, 0x0000000000000000, 0x0000000000000000, 0), HeterogeneousComputing.CPUnit(), ADTypes.NoAutoDiff())))\n\nThe statisics above (mode, mean and std-dev) are presented in shaped form. However, it's not possible to represent statistics with matrix shape, e.g. the parameter covariance matrix, this way. So the covariance has to be accessed in unshaped form:\n\npar_cov = cov(unshaped_samples)\nprintln(\"Covariance: $par_cov\")\nCovariance: [520.1635067123591 -3.953465822168633 -0.043532109718868374 0.0027601086176973918 0.017470555986211576; -3.953465822168633 1000.5032307059794 0.00636070837701951 -0.0028383234390904097 0.0011842048572131226; -0.043532109718868374 0.00636070837701951 0.0006397440873128343 7.757202441907895e-6 -3.8852054344117395e-5; 0.0027601086176973918 -0.0028383234390904097 7.757202441907895e-6 0.0002646102979665645 -2.125999524443925e-6; 0.017470555986211576 0.0011842048572131226 -3.8852054344117395e-5 -2.125999524443925e-6 0.00010245618024125562]\n\nUse LazyReports.lazyreport to generate an overview of the sampling result and parameter estimates (based on the marginal distributions):\n\nusing LazyReports\nlazyreport(samples)\n\u001b[1m  Sampling result\u001b[22m\n  \u001b[1m–––––––––––––––\u001b[22m\n\n    \u001b[34m• \u001b[39m Total number of samples: 94065\n    \u001b[34m• \u001b[39m Total weight of samples: 400000\n    \u001b[34m• \u001b[39m Effective sample size: between 11569 and 15290\n\n\u001b[1m  Marginals\u001b[22m\n  \u001b[1m---------\u001b[22m\n  Parameter Mean     Std. dev. Gobal mode Marg. mode Cred. interval       Histogram                                                 \n  ––––––––– –––––––– ––––––––– –––––––––– –––––––––– –––––––––––––––––––– ––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––\n  a[1]      503.235  22.8071   499.816    510.0      479.477 .. 525.78    ⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀399[⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀▁▂▃▄▆▇████▇▆▅▄▃▂▁⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀[615⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀\n  a[2]      999.816  31.6307   999.669    990.0      967.08 .. 1031.39    ⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀867[⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀▁▁▂▃▄▅▆▇████▇▆▅▄▃▂▁▁⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀[1.13e+03\n  mu[1]     -1.02609 0.0252932 -1.02594   -1.03      -1.05234 .. -1.00091 ⠀⠀⠀-1.13[⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀▁▁▂▃▄▆▇████▇▇▅▄▃▂▁▁⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀[-0.901⠀⠀\n  mu[2]     1.99115  0.0162668 1.99262    1.995      1.97422 .. 2.00737   ⠀⠀⠀⠀1.92[⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀▁▁▂▃▄▅▆▇████▇▆▅▄▃▂▁▁⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀[2.06⠀⠀⠀⠀\n  sigma     0.510164 0.0101221 0.510443   0.5075     multiple             ⠀⠀⠀0.468[⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀▁▂▃▄▅▆▇█████▆▆▅▃▂▂▁⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀[0.554⠀⠀⠀\n","title":"Parameter Space Exploration via MCMC"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Visualization-of-Results","page":"Tutorial","text":"BAT.jl comes with an extensive set of plotting recipes for [\"Plots.jl\"] (http://docs.juliaplots.org/latest/). We can plot the marginalized distribution for a single parameter (e.g. parameter 3, i.e. μ[1]):\n\nplot(\n    samples, :(mu[1]),\n    mean = true, std = true, globalmode = true, marginalmode = true,\n    nbins = 50, title = \"Marginalized Distribution for mu[1]\"\n)\nsavefig(\"tutorial-single-par.pdf\")\n\n(Image: Marginalized Distribution for mu_1)\n\nor plot the marginalized distribution for a pair of parameters (e.g. parameters 3 and 5, i.e. μ[1] and σ), including information from the parameter stats:\n\nplot(\n    samples, (:(mu[1]), :sigma),\n    mean = true, std = true, globalmode = true, marginalmode = true,\n    nbins = 50, title = \"Marginalized Distribution for mu[1] and sigma\"\n)\nplot!(BAT.MCMCBasicStats(samples), (3, 5))\nsavefig(\"tutorial-param-pair.png\")\n\n(Image: Marginalized Distribution for mu_1 and sigma)\n\nWe can also create an overview plot of the marginalized distribution for all pairs of parameters:\n\nplot(\n    samples,\n    mean = false, std = false, globalmode = true, marginalmode = false,\n    nbins = 50\n)\nsavefig(\"tutorial-all-params.png\")\n\n(Image: Pairwise Correlation between Parameters)","title":"Visualization of Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Integration-with-Tables.jl","page":"Tutorial","text":"DensitySamplesVector supports the Tables.jl interface, so it is a table itself. We can also convert it to other table types, e.g. a TypedTables.Table:\n\nusing TypedTables\n\ntbl = Table(samples)\nTable with 5 columns and 94065 rows:\n      v                       logd      weight  info                    aux\n    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n 1  │ (a = [447.867, 1035.8…  -179.237  3       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 2  │ (a = [436.981, 1031.2…  -180.731  1       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 3  │ (a = [443.943, 1032.3…  -180.368  4       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 4  │ (a = [435.477, 1010.4…  -180.135  1       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 5  │ (a = [470.898, 1037.4…  -176.899  1       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 6  │ (a = [468.638, 1043.2…  -177.693  7       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 7  │ (a = [466.982, 1041.9…  -178.33   2       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 8  │ (a = [459.712, 1030.2…  -179.684  4       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 9  │ (a = [491.237, 1063.3…  -179.742  10      MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 10 │ (a = [522.629, 1036.4…  -177.491  2       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 11 │ (a = [488.414, 1032.0…  -177.53   4       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 12 │ (a = [475.005, 1011.5…  -178.617  4       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 13 │ (a = [485.438, 1037.7…  -179.187  3       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 14 │ (a = [474.907, 1040.5…  -180.387  1       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 15 │ (a = [478.361, 1015.2…  -178.147  2       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 16 │ (a = [469.081, 1038.6…  -178.095  4       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n 17 │ (a = [475.427, 1004.3…  -180.64   2       MCMCSampleID(24, 1, 6…  nothing\n ⋮  │           ⋮                ⋮        ⋮               ⋮                ⋮\n\nor a DataFrames.DataFrame, etc.","title":"Integration with Tables.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Comparison-of-Truth-and-Best-Fit","page":"Tutorial","text":"As a final step, we retrieve the parameter values at the mode, representing the best-fit parameters\n\nsamples_mode = mode(samples)\n(a = [499.81606210348536, 999.669293795825], mu = [-1.0259423364383016, 1.9926152133203199], sigma = 0.5104428178799523)\n\nLike the samples themselves, the result can be viewed in both shaped and unshaped form. samples_mode is presented as a 0-dimensional array that contains a NamedTuple, this representation preserves the shape information:\n\nsamples_mode isa NamedTuple\ntrue\n\nsamples_mode is only an estimate of the mode of the posterior distribution. It can be further refined using bat_findmode:\n\nusing Optim\n\nfindmode_result = bat_findmode(\n    posterior,\n    TransformedMaxDensity(optalg = OptimAlg(optalg = Optim.NelderMead()), init = ExplicitInit([samples_mode]))\n)\n\nfit_par_values = findmode_result.result\n(a = [501.871712019655, 998.9129186356382], mu = [-1.0252766402315203, 1.9911561867607674], sigma = 0.5089563901682606)\n\nLet's plot the data and fit function given the true parameters and MCMC samples\n\nplot(-4:0.01:4, fit_function, samples)\n\nplot!(\n    normalize(hist, mode=:density),\n    color=1, linewidth=2, fillalpha=0.0,\n    st = :steps, fill=false, label = \"Data\",\n    title = \"Data, True Model and Best Fit\"\n)\n\nplot!(-4:0.01:4, x -> fit_function(true_par_values, x), color=4, label = \"Truth\")\nsavefig(\"tutorial-data-truth-bestfit.pdf\")\n\n(Image: Data, True Model and Best Fit)","title":"Comparison of Truth and Best Fit"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Fine-grained-control","page":"Tutorial","text":"BAT provides fine-grained control over the MCMC algorithm options, the MCMC chain initialization, tuning/burn-in strategy and convergence testing. All option value used in the following are the default values, any or all may be omitted.\n\nWe'll sample using the random-walk Metropolis-Hastings MCMC algorithm:\n\nmcmcalgo = RandomWalk()\nRandomWalk{Float64, Tuple{Float64, Float64}, Distributions.TDist{Float64}}\n  target_acceptance: Float64 0.234\n  target_acceptance_int: Tuple{Float64, Float64}\n  proposaldist: Distributions.TDist{Float64}\n\n\nBAT requires a counter-based random number generator (RNG), since it partitions the RNG space over the MCMC chains. This way, a single RNG seed is sufficient for all chains and results are reproducible even under parallel execution. By default, BAT uses a Philox4x RNG initialized with a random seed drawn from the system entropy pool:\n\nusing Random123\nrng = Philox4x()\ncontext = BATContext(rng = Philox4x())\n\nBy default, RandomWalk() uses the following options.\n\nFor Markov chain initialization:\n\ninit = MCMCChainPoolInit()\nMCMCChainPoolInit\n  init_tries_per_chain: IntervalSets.ClosedInterval{Int64}\n  nsteps_init: Int64 1000\n  initval_alg: InitFromTarget InitFromTarget()\n  strict: Bool true\n\n\nFor the MCMC burn-in procedure:\n\nburnin = MCMCMultiCycleBurnin()\nMCMCMultiCycleBurnin\n  nsteps_per_cycle: Int64 10000\n  max_ncycles: Int64 30\n  nsteps_final: Int64 1000\n\n\nFor convergence testing:\n\nconvergence = BrooksGelmanConvergence()\nBrooksGelmanConvergence\n  threshold: Float64 1.1\n  corrected: Bool false\n\n\nTo generate MCMC samples with explicit control over all options, use something like\n\nsamples = bat_sample(\n    posterior,\n    TransformedMCMC(\n        proposal = mcmcalgo,\n        nchains = 4,\n        nsteps = 10^5,\n        init = init,\n        burnin = burnin,\n        convergence = convergence,\n        strict = true,\n        store_burnin = false,\n        nonzero_weights = true,\n        callback = (x...) -> nothing\n    ),\n    context\n).result\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMCChainPoolInit: trying to generate 4 viable MCMC chain state(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mSelected 4 MCMC chain state(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mBegin tuning of 4 MCMC chain(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMC Tuning cycle 1 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 4 converged.\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mMCMC tuning of 4 chains successful after 1 cycle(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mRunning post-tuning stabilization steps for 4 MCMC chain(s).\n\u001b[36m\u001b[1m[ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mInfo: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mGenerate main samples using 4 MCMC chain(s).","title":"Fine-grained control"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Saving-result-data-to-files","page":"Tutorial","text":"The package FileIO.jl(in conjunction with JLD2.jl) offers a convenient way to store results like posterior samples to file:\n\nusing FileIO\nimport JLD2\nFileIO.save(\"results.jld2\", Dict(\"samples\" => samples))\n\nJLD2 persists the full information (including value shapes), so you can reload exactly the same data into memory in a new Julia session via\n\nusing FileIO\nimport JLD2\nsamples = FileIO.load(\"results.jld2\", \"samples\")\n\nprovided you use compatible versions of BAT and it's dependencies. Note that JLD2 is not a long-term stable file format. Also note that this functionality is provided by FileIO.jl and JLD2.jl and not part of the BAT API itself.\n\nBAT.jl itself can write samples to standard HDF5 files in a form suitable for long-term storage (via HDF5.jl):\n\nimport HDF5\nbat_write(\"results.h5\", samples)\n\nThe resulting files have an intuitive HDF5 layout and can be read with the standard HDF5 libraries, so they are easily accessible from other programming languages as well. Not all value shape information can be preserved, though. To read BAT.jl HDF5 sample data, use\n\nusing BAT\nimport HDF5\nsamples = bat_read(\"results.h5\").result\n\nBAT.jl's HDF5 file format may evolve over time, but future versions of BAT.jl will be able to read HDF5 sample data written by this version of BAT.jl.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Saving result data to files"},{"category":"section","location":"#BAT.jl-Documentation","page":"Home","text":"BAT.jl is a Bayesian Analysis Toolkit in Julia. It is a high-performance tool box for Bayesian inference with statistical models expressed in a general-purpose programming language instead of a domain-specific language.\n\nTypical applications for this package are parameter inference given a model (in the form of a likelihood function and prior), the comparison of different models in the light of a given data set, and the test of the validity of a model to represent the data set at hand. BAT.jl provides access to the full Bayesian posterior distribution to enable parameter estimation, limit setting and uncertainty propagation. BAT.jl also provides supporting functionality like plotting recipes and reporting functions.\n\nBAT.jl is implemented in pure Julia and allows for a flexible definition of mathematical models and applications while enabling the user to code for the performance required for computationally expensive numerical operations. BAT.jl provides implementations (internally and via other Julia packages) of algorithms for sampling, optimization and integration. BAT's main focus is on the analysis of complex custom models. It is designed to enable parallel code execution at various levels (running multiple MCMC chains in parallel is provided out-of-the-box).\n\nIt's possible to use BAT.jl with likelihood functions implemented in languages other than Julia: Julia allows for calling code in C and Fortran, C++, Python and several other languages directly.\n\nBAT.jl originated as a rewrite/redesign of BAT, the Bayesian Analysis Toolkit in C++. BAT.jl now offer a different set of functionality and a wider variety of algorithms than it's C++ predecessor.\n\nnote: Note\nBAT.jl requires Julia >= v1.10, we recommend to use the latest stable Julia version for optimal performance.","title":"BAT.jl Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Table-of-contents","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\n    # \"basics.md\",\n    \"installation.md\",\n    \"tutorial.md\",\n    # \"faq.md\",\n    # \"examples.md\",\n    # \"algorithms.md\",\n    # \"benchmarks.md\",\n    # \"publications.md\",\n    \"api.md\",\n    \"developing.md\",\n    \"license.md\",\n]\nDepth = 1","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing-BAT.jl","page":"Home","text":"When using BAT.jl for research, teaching or similar, please cite Schulz et al. \"BAT.jl: A Julia-Based Tool for Bayesian Inference\", SNCS (2021).\n\n@article{Schulz:2021BAT,\n  author  = {Schulz, Oliver and Beaujean, Frederik and Caldwell, Allen and Grunwald, Cornelius and Hafych, Vasyl and Kr{\\\"o}ninger, Kevin and Cagnina, Salvatore La and R{\\\"o}hrig, Lars and Shtembari, Lolian},\n  journal = {SN Computer Science},\n  title   = {BAT.jl: A Julia-Based Tool for Bayesian Inference},\n  year    = {2021},\n  issn    = {2661-8907},\n  month   = {Apr},\n  number  = {3},\n  pages   = {210},\n  volume  = {2},\n  day     = {12},\n  doi     = {10.1007/s42979-021-00626-4},\n  url     = {https://doi.org/10.1007/s42979-021-00626-4},\n}","title":"Citing BAT.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Learning-(more-about)-Julia","page":"Home","text":"BAT.jl supersedes BAT in C++. If you're considering to switch to BAT.jl, but you're new to Julia and want to learn more about the the language, here are a few resources to get started:\n\nThe Julia website provides many links to introductory videos and written tutorials, e.g. \"Intro to Julia\", Think Julia: How to Think Like a Computer Scientist and \"The Fast Track to Julia\". If you are familiar with MATLAB or Python, you may also want to take a look at the \"MATLAB–Python–Julia cheatsheet\".\n\nThe in-depth article Why Numba and Cython are not substitutes for Julia explains how Julia addresses several fundamental challenges inherent to scientific high-performance computing.","title":"Learning (more about) Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"Home","text":"We acknowledge the contributions from all the BAT.jl users, they help us make BAT.jl a better project. Your help is most welcome!\n\nDevelopment of BAT.jl has been supported by funding from\n\nDeutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG, German Research Foundation)\nEuropean Union Framework Programme for Research and Innovation Horizon 2020 (2014-2020) under the Marie Sklodowska-Curie Grant Agreement No.765710","title":"Acknowledgements"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Experimental-features","page":"Experimental Features","text":"These are experimental features. Forward/backward compatibility does not follow Julia's semantic versioning rules. Instead, compatibility is only guaranteed across changes in patch version, but not across changes of minor (or major) version.\n\nThe features listed here are likely to transition to the stable API in future versions, but may still evolve in a API-breaking fashion during that process.","title":"Experimental features"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Interactive-static-Makie-figures","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_makie_plot returns the Makie.Figure it constructs, including its controls. By default it also displays that same figure:\n\nfig = bat_makie_plot(samples);\n\nTo defer display, for example when saving from a script, use:\n\nfig = bat_makie_plot(samples; display=false);\nsave(\"samples.png\", fig)\n\ndisplay=false skips BAT's explicit display and optional shader warmup. It still constructs the Makie figure and evaluates the plotting graph; saving or displaying later requires a suitable backend. It does not suppress automatic display of the returned value by a REPL, notebook, or IDE. The semicolons above suppress that extra output. For automatic inline output, use a final expression such as bat_makie_plot(samples; display=false) without a semicolon.\n\nThe variable picker in static and live figures shows the first ten coordinates. Use Clear selection to remove all selected variables, including higher coordinates supplied through vsel, then choose replacements in the picker. Open the controls with ☰ to access the picker and its clear button.\n\nThe return changed from nothing to Makie.Figure. Existing explicit display is preserved, but return-value checks, automatic frontend output, and collections of results can change behavior or retain figures. This is an experimental API change, subject to the compatibility policy at the top of this page.\n\nThe result supports normal Makie figure operations. Its internal BAT graph and control callbacks are not public API. Interactive grid updates follow the state policy below. Ordinary Makie.plot composition retains its existing interface; the display keyword above belongs to bat_makie_plot. Live sessions retain the separate lifetime operations described below.\n\nCalling empty!(fig) also disconnects BAT's static control subscriptions. A retained control no longer updates the old plotting graph after its figure is emptied.\n\nIf static construction or BAT's explicit display fails after allocating a figure, BAT attempts to release its content and owned display resources before rethrowing the original error.","title":"Interactive static Makie figures"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Ordinary-Makie-composition","page":"Experimental Features","text":"Use Makie.plot to place BAT samples in a caller-owned figure or layout position:\n\nusing BAT, Makie, CairoMakie\n\nsamples = DensitySampleVector([[0.2, 0.3], [0.5, 0.8], [0.9, 0.6]], zeros(3))\nfig = Figure()\nMakie.plot(fig[1, 1], samples; vsel = [1, 2], N_max = 2);\n\nThe BAT-specific conversion keywords are recipes, vsel, N_max, trace_nsteps, support, step_intervals and dark. Their defaults match bat_makie_plot, but recipes, vsel and N_max are passed as keywords here. vsel currently requires a Vector of integers; N_max is a positive integer. The display keyword belongs to bat_makie_plot, not this conversion.\n\nThis path produces the corner-plot layout without BAT's interactive menus, variable picker or Apply/All controls. It uses the caller's theme and Makie's layout/reconciliation behavior. dark chooses contrast-dependent BAT overlay colors; it does not install the BAT dark theme. Apply bat_theme or bat_theme_dark through Makie's own scoped theme operations if desired.\n\nThe same support and timing declarations are accepted. step_intervals supplies trace timing; it does not add an interactive range selector to ordinary plots. See Sample ranges and repetition timing, KDE support and credible regions, and the caller-owned update-failure policy under Histogram weights and statistical overlays.","title":"Ordinary Makie composition"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Sample-ranges-and-repetition-timing","page":"Experimental Features","text":"Without timing, the range selects stored samples per walker, preserving their weights. To declare exact repetition timing, supply step_intervals: one vector per sample row, containing (cycle, first, last) named tuples with inclusive, positive step endpoints. A row may have intervals in several cycles. Durations must sum to that row's repetition weight, and intervals within a chain/walker/ cycle must not overlap. For example, weights [100, 1] can be accompanied by [[(cycle=1, first=1, last=100)], [(cycle=1, first=101, last=101)]]. The selected local step range applies independently to every cycle and clips weights to the overlap. Bare sample metadata does not certify this timing. The same keyword is supported by ordinary Makie plot(samples; ...). The slider uses bounded ticks for large ranges; enter exact start:end values in the range field and press Enter, then Apply, for precise selection.\n\nElapsed-step traces require explicit timing. ARP and other statistical weights are not interpreted as dwell durations; use the stored-sample range instead.\n\nFor plots with declared step timing, selected repetition counts must be exactly representable by the samples' weight type. A window that would round a count raises an error; use integer repetition weights for exact long step ranges.","title":"Sample ranges and repetition timing"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Histogram-weights-and-statistical-overlays","page":"Experimental Features","text":"Histogram :probability and :pdf normalization and Makie population moments use temporary relative weights. Common finite weight scaling therefore preserves their results within the supported numerical range. Stored samples and declared step counts remain unchanged. Inputs must have finite nonnegative weights and finite coordinates wherever weight is positive. Positive mass that would be lost in the working precision raises an error. Empty or zero-mass Makie populations retain empty-display behavior; a MarginalDist requires positive in-bin mass.\n\nScatter marker areas use relative sample weights, normalized before conversion to Float64. Finite BigFloat and logarithmic weights therefore retain their relative sizes when the ratios are representable. Unrepresentable positive ratios raise an error. ChainScatter2D requires chain identity for every sample.\n\nHistogram :none retains counts; :density divides counts by bin volume without turning them into probabilities. These modes, report counts and native hexbin weights require a representable absolute total. Overflow raises an error even if each bin could individually hold its count. Legacy Plots mean/spread overlays also retain absolute frequency-weight arithmetic and its correction; a corrected spread requires total weight greater than one. Only requested overlays and their selected coordinates are evaluated.\n\nDensity division avoids intermediate products of widths and total mass. The result still must fit the numerical and rendering types actually used. Hist2D color heights must survive Makie's Float32 conversion and admit a usable default linear automatic color range, including its span and reciprocal arithmetic. This admission applies before native styling: a custom color range or color scale does not rescue rejected heights. Admitted inputs retain native styling; nearby heights may still become indistinguishable colors. Change normalization or units, or use QuantileHist2D when a limit is reached. ARP weights can reach these limits even with ordinary coordinate ranges. Statistical overlays reject nonfinite or collapsed positive spread and interval geometry. Constant data and singular covariance remain supported where the recipe defines them. Covariance decompositions must reproduce the covariance in each coordinate's scale before an ellipse is drawn; inaccurate decompositions raise an error.\n\nHist1D bar widths come from the original bin edges, including single-bin histograms. A BarPlot.width theme cannot override those widths. Native gap, dodge, color and label styling remains available. The extension checks the actual native bar endpoints against exact edge-based layout arithmetic. Each endpoint's displacement is limited to 32eps(Float32) times the intended styled bin width; intentionally zero-width styling uses the original bin width as its scale. This bounds relative width error by 64eps(Float32) for nonzero styled bars, before backend transforms and rasterization. Gap/dodge styling can intentionally change the displayed area; it does not change the histogram's stored heights.\n\nVery narrow bins at large coordinate offsets can fail this check even with finite, distinct edges. Such layouts raise an ArgumentError, possibly wrapped by Makie's compute pipeline, rather than silently shifting or collapsing bins. Recenter the coordinates or use the original-edge QuantileHist1D representation. Uniformly rescaling coordinates need not improve their offset-to-width ratio.\n\nHexbin2D colors show absolute weighted counts, with thresholds in the same units. The Makie extension requires Makie 0.24.8 or later in the 0.24 series, for native Hexbin count nodes and safe transient widget layouts. After native bin aggregation and threshold filtering, displayed counts must survive Float32 conversion without materially changing their normalized color positions. Unsupported magnitudes, collapsed ranges, or insufficient color resolution raise an ArgumentError (possibly wrapped by Makie's compute pipeline). Empty and uniform-count displays retain Makie's automatic range policy. Tiny individual weights are allowed when their displayed bin totals pass this check.\n\nRescaling count units and thresholds together may resolve magnitude limits; it need not repair insufficient relative contrast. A normalized histogram or whole-bin region recipe is another option. Admission bounds scalar color positions within 8eps(Float32) under CPU/Float32 arithmetic, including reciprocal-based normalization and subnormal subtraction flushed to zero. The reciprocal span must remain normal with headroom for shader arithmetic. This does not promise distinct pixels for every distinct count or identical palette interpolation across renderers. Colorbar endpoints retain absolute count units at Float32 precision.\n\nBAT-owned interactive updates demand these histogram/hexbin checks before returning to a renderer. Ordinary caller-owned Makie plots may reject on update or later geometry/color evaluation. After a rejected update, dispose and recreate the caller-owned figure; in-place retry is not supported. Live BAT visualization follows its existing failure and disposal policy and marks the presentation incomplete.\n\nMarginalDist checks its dependency's actual PDF and reconstructed bin masses. Its dependency's cached coordinate moments have separate extreme-range limits. Histogram regions rank cells by density and accumulate integrated cell mass. For count/probability histograms the stored heights are masses; for density/PDF histograms the mass is height times actual bin volume. Regions preserve the original displayed heights and include the entire cutoff-density plateau. This intentionally replaces first-bin tie breaking. They are whole-bin highest-density regions: achieved probability can exceed the request, sometimes substantially, and the result is not a globally minimum-volume selection of whole bins.\n\nThe internal central-region helper uses actual left/right tail masses and retains a boundary bin when the removed tail would exactly equal its target. Zero requests are empty, full-mass requests retain every positive cell, and zero-mass histograms remain empty displays. Makie histogram levels use zero as an inactive sentinel and support up to three positive requests in (0,1]; invalid levels are errors. Legacy Plots region labels report achieved probability. Custom labels and colors stay associated with their requests when requests are reordered.\n\nRegion arithmetic factors volumes and densities before common power-of-two mass scaling. Each positive cell must retain positive relative mass; otherwise it raises an error. Region helpers accept ordinary integer/floating-point histogram storage, with endpoints exactly representable in the promoted Float64 or BigFloat working type. Arithmetic must use round-to-nearest and preserve subnormals; BigFloat inputs at its minimum exponent boundary are rejected. These checks do not change the caller's arithmetic mode. Finite arithmetic can change a whole boundary plateau near a probability crossing or nearly tied computed density; it does not guarantee identical masks under every floating-point rescaling. Whole-bin regions use original edges, including horizontal fills and unequal 2D cells.\n\nLegacy Plots contours are approximate isolines of an interpolated density field, not exact boundaries whose enclosed probability equals the selected cell mass. Their cutoffs use the same relative-density field that is displayed. Smoothing uses bin-index units and zero padding/cropping, then recomputes cell probabilities within that displayed domain; it is not uniform physical-space smoothing on unequal bins. Contours require at least two cells on each axis and a representable positive relative-density field, with distinct Float64 centers inside their cells, even when exact cell regions are representable. Coincident cutoffs retain the tighter request's color. Numeric contour labels cannot be interpreted as original density units.\n\nContour smoothing accepts a finite, nonnegative real scalar or a two-tuple (sigma_x, sigma_y). A scalar applies to both axes; zero disables smoothing on that axis. Each axis uses Gaussian support radius 2*ceil(sigma) after conversion to the Float64 working type. Active smoothing is admitted before constructing its kernel or padded input, with per-contour limits of 2^20 kernel cells, 2^23 additional Float64 numeric-buffer slots (64 MiB payload), and 2^26 direct convolution contributions. Gaussian offset squares must also fit the native integer type; this further restricts wide one-axis kernels on 32-bit hosts. The buffer limit includes the kernel, padded input, coefficient vectors and numerical temporaries; it excludes existing histogram storage, preceding region preparation, object/runtime overhead and rendering. It is not a total allocation or process-memory bound. For example, a 200×200 grid admits scalar sigma 9 but rejects sigma 10 on work cost. Excessive requests raise ArgumentError rather than changing the requested smoothing. Reduce sigma or the number of bins, or select whole-bin regions. Empty/all-zero probability requests and zero-mass histograms require no smoothing and accept any finite, nonnegative sigma; sign and finiteness are still validated.","title":"Histogram weights and statistical overlays"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#KDE-support-and-credible-regions","page":"Experimental Features","text":"For KDE1D, QuantileKDE1D, KDE2D and QuantileKDE2D, the support keyword declares hard support in the original sample coordinates. Both bat_makie_plot(samples; support=prior, display=false) and ordinary Makie.plot(samples; support=prior) accept a measure/prior or a vector of per-dimension (lo, hi) pairs. Live plots obtain these bounds from the original target/prior, including when it is wrapped in an EvaluatedMeasure. Unknown distribution support is conservatively treated as unbounded. Axis limits and observed extrema do not declare hard support.\n\nBounds must be strictly ordered, without NaN, incorrectly directed infinities, or finite endpoints outside Float64's representable range. Positive-weight observations must be finite and lie inside the original support bounds before coordinates and bounds are converted to Float64 for the numerical grid; they are never silently clamped. Bounds must retain positive width after conversion. Weights must be finite and nonnegative. Zero-weight observations are excluded before bandwidth and extent selection, so a zero-weight outlier cannot widen an estimate. Empty or all-zero populations have no density to display. If Float16/Float32 division would discard a positive relative weight that is representable in Float64, KDE admission raises an error; convert the weights to Float64 first. Relative weights genuinely below Float64 representation remain excluded from the convolution population.\n\nUnbounded estimates retain the existing bandwidth rule and fixed FFT grid. Admission checks the selected bandwidth against centered, scaled calculations, including bounds on weighted quartile ranks and coordinate conversion. Constant populations also require reliable effective-count arithmetic. The actual Float64 grid and cell edges, convolution arithmetic, and raw density mass are checked. The Float64 FFT buffer range is checked even when input arithmetic uses BigFloat. Numerically ambiguous effective sample counts or bandwidths, large offsets relative to grid spacing, and unsafe coordinate units raise an error before any visibility mask or credible-region calculation. Convert coordinates and weights to Float64, recenter or change units, or use a histogram. Accepted estimates are not rescaled or replaced by a different estimator. These conservative admission checks do not remove the inherited fixed-grid approximation or four-bandwidth periodic tails.\n\nBounded estimates also retain their selected bandwidth. Admission checks it against a calculation in local coordinates, including bounds on quartile-rank arithmetic, coordinate conversion and its effect on reference spread. True constants and singletons retain their absolute fallback. Unresolved variance, lost reference deviations, uncertain quartiles or unreliable bandwidths raise an error. An unavailable unequal-weight bandwidth no longer falls back to an unweighted rule; an effective count rounded to one is an arithmetic limit. Convert coordinates and weights to Float64, recenter or change units, or use a histogram. These checks do not replace the estimator or promise exact arithmetic at every numerical boundary.\n\nBoundary correction uses one Gaussian mirror per nearby finite boundary, including corner images in 2D, selected within four bandwidths. This is a finite reflection approximation, not an infinite reflection series. The FFT interval follows the data and included images, with extra padding; a distant support endpoint does not stretch the numerical grid. The clipped result is normalized within its returned interval or cells. Unsupported numerical ranges or a grid exceeding the internal resolution/memory budget raise an error rather than returning an unrestricted or silently coarsened density. A histogram is an alternative for these populations. These limits apply per estimate, not to total figure memory or rendering latency.\n\nMakie stores numeric heatmap color values as Float32. KDE2D therefore rejects visible densities that would overflow, become zero or subnormal, or leave an invalid native color span or reciprocal, even if the numerical estimate is valid in Float64. This includes uniform densities whose automatic range collapses after Float32 rounding. Change coordinate units or use QuantileKDE2D, which passes credible-region colors directly to Makie. Density values are never silently rescaled to hide this rendering limit.\n\nIn 1D the displayed density is a piecewise-linear curve, with interpolated endpoints where it meets hard support. QuantileKDE1D integrates this curve to find highest-density regions, including interpolated threshold crossings; separated modes can produce disconnected filled regions. In 2D the displayed density consists of constant-density cells with explicit edges inside support. QuantileKDE2D ranks cells by density and accumulates density times actual cell area. Both region recipes include complete threshold ties, so achieved content can exceed the requested content. A single constant-density cell belongs to every nonzero requested region. The ordinary KDE2D visibility mask is applied after normalization and does not redefine probability mass.\n\nOrdinary all-positive unbounded density estimates retain their existing estimation path where valid. The improved integrated 1D region calculation can change their credible-region thresholds and polygons. Excluding zero weights from bandwidth selection is also an intentional correction.","title":"KDE support and credible regions"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Interactive-grid-updates","page":"Experimental Features","text":"Interactive static and live figures keep their axes in fixed grid cells while recipes, samples and selected variables change. Inactive cells are collapsed; recipe round trips retain Figure membership and working controls. Plot object identity and the nesting of recipe internals are implementation details.\n\nBAT owns the grid's labels, decorations, aspect and computed bounds. Other axis styling, such as titles and background colors, stays with its grid position. Across BAT updates, navigation and explicit limits! overrides survive while that cell represents the same variables with unchanged finite computed bounds. A variable/activation change or changed bounds resets the whole cell, including both coordinates when a diagonal density height changes. Automatic dimensions are recomputed after updates. Removed variables have no saved navigation state. Makie's display and export operations can independently reset pan/zoom; this policy does not override those operations.\n\nGenerated coordinate bounds include the original bin intervals of the selected histogram recipes, in addition to the padded data domain. This preserves complete outer bins without changing their statistical edges or density heights. For unchanged observation history, changing the sample window, including an empty window, leaves these coordinate bounds unchanged. Live sample publication can change the data domain and therefore the coordinate bounds. Switching recipes can change the required bounds and reset navigation under the rule above. Caller zoom or explicit limits may still crop bins intentionally.\n\nThese rules apply to BAT's interactive figures. Ordinary Makie.plot composition uses the caller's existing Makie layout and reconciliation behavior.","title":"Interactive grid updates"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Live-Makie-configuration","page":"Experimental Features","text":"See BATMakieVisualization for constructors, fields and defaults. The following guarantees apply to its live observation pipeline.\n\nPrior-based plot bounds use a private copy of the sampling context's RNG; estimating them does not advance the sampling RNG or the default RNG.\n\nBatching and backpressure count completed walker-steps across all chains, including rejected steps. Terminal sampler flushes contribute no steps. Repetition-weighted runs capture cycle/step timing without changing returned samples. Apply selects local steps in every cycle and clips displayed dwell weights; All follows the growing data. If timing is unavailable (including ARP weighting), the labelled sample range preserves each selected row's weight and elapsed-step traces are unavailable. Slider movement is a draft until Apply. For long runs the slider uses bounded ticks; edit start:end and press Enter to set exact integer endpoints, then Apply to update the plots. max_buffered must be at least the batch size (50 with the keyword constructor). It is an initial backpressure threshold, not a strict memory limit: a multi-walker update may cross it before waiting. With adaptive batching, both thresholds grow by the finite batch_growth_rate > 1, saturating at typemax(Int).\n\nobserver_timeout (default 60 seconds) bounds each continuous backpressure wait and, separately, finalization. Exceeding it disables observation while sampling continues. Both it and poll_interval must be finite and positive. Timeouts require scheduler progress; they cannot cancel a stuck native renderer. Inspect BAT.visualizer_status(vis) for incomplete observation or pending cleanup, and call BAT.dispose_visualizer!(vis) to release a retained completed view.","title":"Live Makie configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental_api/#Live-Makie-session-lifetime","page":"Experimental Features","text":"Create a fresh BATVisualizer(BATMakieVisualization(...)) for each sampling run. Successful completion drains the final observations and retains an interactive figure. Closing the live figure disables observation while sampling continues. To release a retained figure and its observation history, call BAT.dispose_visualizer!(vis) on the thread that created it. This operation requests disposal without joining active view callbacks. Cleanup waits for admitted startup, listener and view work, then releases the session's graph and computed caches without requiring status polling. It also releases a fresh session that has never created a figure; disposed sessions remain single-use. Inspect BAT.visualizer_status(vis).cleanup_pending if needed. A cleanup fault keeps release pending; another owner-thread disposal call can retry it. BAT's GLMakie display, renderer warmup and live initialization must run on Julia's main thread. BAT creates a separate native screen for each newly displayed figure, preserving unrelated windows, and reuses that figure's open screen on repeated display. BAT scopes its theme to figure construction, before renderer startup. Custom renderer callbacks must not wait on locks held by their caller, including an outer caller-owned with_theme scope.\n\nKeep the original visualizer to retrieve its completed figure:\n\nvis = BATVisualizer(BATMakieVisualization())\ncontext = BATContext(visualizer=vis)\nresult = bat_sample(target, algorithm, context)\nfig = BAT.visualizer_figure(vis);\nif !isnothing(fig)\n    save(\"live-samples.png\", fig)\nend\n\nThe getter returns the same retained Makie.Figure once observation has completed successfully and its tasks have settled. It returns nothing during sampling, inside view callbacks, while the view is busy, or when no usable completed figure remains. It does not wait, create a view, display or render. Obtain and use the figure on the thread that created the session; a wrong-thread query raises ArgumentError when the session can be inspected. Control-lock contention returns nothing without waiting.\n\nThis is a borrowed reference. Coordinate saving or customization with view callbacks and disposal; a later close or BAT.dispose_visualizer!(vis) can empty the same figure. Retrieval does not transfer ownership or protect subsequent operations. A deep-copied visualizer, including the sampling result's provenance context, has no live resources and returns nothing. The figure retains the existing live observation population, which can include initialization and burn-in; it is not a reconstructed plot of the returned posterior samples. Availability follows the current completion and view state; an older diagnostic alone does not prevent retrieval. Use BAT.visualizer_status(vis) to inspect it.\n\nLive plots use the original model's flattened value shape, including when the sampler's transformation changes the number of coordinates. For example, a three-component Dirichlet displays all three components although sampling uses two unconstrained coordinates. Declared constant fields do not occupy plot dimensions. This also applies when no initialization samples are retained; initializing an empty display does not add observations or sample weight.\n\nLive figures identify this population with the caption “Observed sampling history” and the note “May include initialization and burn-in”. The caption remains visible when controls are collapsed and in exports of the complete Figure. Applying a range changes the displayed window within that history; it does not select a sampling phase. Static plots of supplied samples make no such provenance claim.\n\nAfter successful startup, a failure in observation or rendering disables further observation without changing fixed-work samples, computational RNG states, or public sampling callbacks. Configuration, reuse, and startup errors still throw; sampling errors and intentional interruption remain errors. Time-limited runs remain sensitive to observation overhead. Direct scalar iteration does not own the session and does not promise a final display flush on cancellation/time-limit exits. For live GLMakie sessions, BAT observes the native render loop it starts even after sampling completes. An error propagated by that loop invalidates the presentation and requests asynchronous disposal; the figure getter then returns nothing. Previously confirmed data_complete and completed observation outcome are preserved, with the rendering error reported separately. Custom render loops must remain running through live startup. An already running caller-owned loop cannot be adopted, and returning while the owned window remains open without a stop request is rejected. Later caller-driven renderer replacements and errors suppressed inside a custom loop are outside this guarantee. When reusing a native screen for another figure, dispose the old BAT session: screen reuse alone can retain old scene callbacks and does not release the session's observation history. Disposal preserves a screen now showing another figure.\n\nBAT-created observation tasks use private RNG state, preserving the initiating task's ordinary draws and the streams of its future child tasks. BAT's GL shader warmup renders without a background loop. Its explicit GL display starts the renderer through a private startup task, joined before returning or cleaning up an interrupted startup. Synchronous scene and inline-display callbacks still execute on the caller, and retain their random draws and child tasks. Renderer callbacks execute in the renderer task. BAT does not rewind a whole callback or display call to hide its effects; private renderer seeds do not promise unique or statistically independent streams for custom rendering code. Direct caller-owned Makie/GLMakie display calls, including later displaying a figure returned with display=false, are outside this BAT startup guarantee. Automatic garbage-collection finalizers are also outside the tested boundary. Initial observation transforms run sequentially on the sampling owner to avoid launching additional row-processing tasks. Custom transforms, including shape and Jacobian probes, retain their own random draws and task creation; their effects are outside the observation neutrality guarantee. Runtime-owned lazy scheduler initialization is also outside this guarantee: on Julia 1.13.2, a thread's first blocking wait can itself change future child streams, even without BAT observation. Failed or aborted live views are disposed; their outcome remains available through the status query and diagnostics independently of the renderer. Disposal after the sampling owner has acquired the session also permits that same acquisition to finish numerical initialization and continue sampling without starting a view. Observation history may already have been released at that point. This continuation is single-use and restricted to the acquiring task and thread; a disposed session cannot be acquired for a new run. Numerical failures and non-benign startup failures still propagate.\n\nCancellation of an MCMC chain group is cooperative. If its sampling owner is interrupted or a chain fails, BAT requests cancellation and releases participating observation waits, then joins every launched chain worker before propagating the error. Initialization timing cleanup consequently follows the last chain writer. Repeated catchable interrupts during this cleanup do not abandon workers or replace the original owner exception. An already-entered numerical step or user hook must finish or throw before its worker can stop; there is no safe forced termination of arbitrary native model code. This ownership guarantee concerns BAT's MCMC chain groups, not arbitrary tasks started by user code or separate parallel sample transformations. It relies on a functioning Julia runtime and the application's signal policy; it cannot cover process termination.\n\nobserver_timeout=60.0 bounds each continuous capacity wait and, separately, the owner's shutdown wait. Nested or repeated cleanup shares the same allowance until the requested resources settle. Expiry stops observation and reports incompleteness; it does not cancel sampling. The allowance uses monotonic time and requires Julia's scheduler to make progress. It cannot interrupt a non-yielding native renderer. A listener still running after a timeout stays owned and reports quiesced=false and cleanup_pending=true until it actually finishes.\n\nBAT.visualizer_status(vis) is independent of rendering. outcome describes the run, data_complete describes confirmed observation history, and last_publication counts successfully returned data publications. Closing a completed figure preserves its historical completeness. A later view error may invalidate presentation_valid independently. error preserves the first failure; cleanup_error records a separate cleanup fault. Each diagnostic is an inert (type, message, phase) snapshot, with a bounded copied type name and message. Plain ErrorException and ArgumentError string messages are retained within the bound; other exceptions use a fallback message. Status and cleanup never call custom error formatters. Diagnostic snapshots do not retain raw exceptions, backtraces or failed tasks. Pending cleanup still owns the tasks it must join. Existing error logging can expose the original exception transiently. Failed sessions do not retry detached batches or resume as if observation had remained complete.\n\nhistory_released becomes true only after disposal has released all session-owned observation history and the cleanup task has terminated successfully. It remains false after successful sampling until disposal, and while a requested release is pending. A no-op or provenance-only visualizer has no such history and reports true. quiesced describes observer resources, not completion of numerical sampling. Retaining only the borrowed, emptied figure does not retain BAT's observation history. Independently retained graph nodes, controls, sampling results, user configuration or logger records can still own data; disposal does not invalidate those external references.","title":"Live Makie session lifetime"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.ARPWeighting","page":"Experimental Features","text":"ARPWeighting{T<:AbstractFloat} <: AbstractMCMCWeightingScheme{T}\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nSample weighting scheme suitable for accept/reject-based sampling algorithms (e.g. RandomWalk). Both accepted and rejected samples become part of the output, with a weight proportional to their original acceptance probability.\n\nConstructors:\n\nARPWeighting()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ARPWeighting"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.batalgorithm","page":"Experimental Features","text":"batalgorithm(algorithm)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nMap algorithm to its BAT equivalent.\n\nWraps backend configurations and third-party algorithms in the BAT algorithm that uses them. Acts as the identity on BAT algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.batalgorithm"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.bat_compare","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_compare(\n    samples_1::DensitySampleVector,\n    samples_2::DensitySampleVector;\n    nsamples::Symbol=:effective\n)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nCompares two DensitySampleVectors given by samples_1 and samples_2 applying the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test for all marginals.\n\nSee N. Smirnov, \"Table for Estimating the Goodness of Fit of Empirical Distributions\" (1948).\n\nnsamples specifies how to define a number of samples in the Kolmogorov-Smirnov distribution. The default value is nsamples=:effective, which uses the effective number of samples estimated by bat_eff_sample_size. The optimal keywords:\n\n:length — each sample vector's own row count, including zero-weight rows.\n:weights — the original absolute weight sum, which must be a positive integer representable as Int. Integer, rational and ordinary binary floating frequency weights are supported. Fractional wider floats must be exactly representable as Float64; logarithmic count encodings are not supported. The total is checked exactly, without normalizing or rounding it.\n:nested — legacy mode: population 1 uses estimated ESS; population 2 uses round(nested_eff_ss / number_of_free_parameters) for every marginal.\n\nEstimated ESS values are rounded to positive Int counts; a scalar estimate is shared by all marginals. Inputs must have at least one free parameter, nonempty populations, finite coordinates, and finite nonnegative weights with positive total mass. Relative CDF weights use at least Float64 precision; unrepresentable positive relative masses are rejected. Gradual underflow and round-to-nearest BigFloat arithmetic with at least 53 bits are required.\n\nThe distance compares complete tied-coordinate groups and is invariant to independent positive weight scaling within floating precision. This does not make :weights sample counts or p-values scale invariant. P-values use the asymptotic continuous-null KS distribution with the selected counts; ties, correlation or arbitrary importance weights do not have exact calibration.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = X::TypedTables.Table, ...)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_compare"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.bat_integrated_autocorr_len","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_integrated_autocorr_len(\n    v::_ACLenTarget,\n    algorithm::AutocorLenAlgorithm = GeyerAutocorLen(),\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nEstimate the integrated autocorrelation length of variate series v, separately for each degree of freedom.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = integrated_autocorr_len, ...)\n\nResult properties not listed here are algorithm-specific and are not part of the stable public API.\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add methods to bat_integrated_autocorr_len, add methods to bat_integrated_autocorr_len_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_integrated_autocorr_len"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.bat_marginalmode","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_marginalmode(\n    target::DensitySampleVector,\n    algorithm::AbstractModeEstimator,\n    [context::BATContext]\n)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nEstimates a marginal mode of target by finding the maximum of marginalized posterior for each dimension.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple of the shape\n\n(result = v, ...)\n\nnote: Note\nDo not add add methods to bat_marginalmode, add methods to bat_marginalmode_impl instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_marginalmode"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.auto_renormalize","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.auto_renormalize(measure::MeasureBase.AbstractMeasure)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturns (result = new_measure, logweight = logweight).\n\nTries to automatically renormalize measure if a maximum log-density value is available, returns measure unchanged otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.auto_renormalize"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.BinnedModeEstimator","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BinnedMarginalModes <: AbstractModeEstimator\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nBin data to estimate modes.\n\nConstructor: BinnedModeEstimator(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nbinning::Any: Default: FreedmanDiaconisBinning()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BinnedModeEstimator"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.convert_for","page":"Experimental Features","text":"convert_for(function, obj)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nConvert obj into something that function can use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.convert_for"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.DistributionTransform","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type DistributionTransform <: Function\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nTransform variate values between distributions\n\nConstructors:\n\nDistributionTransform(target_dist, source_dist)\nDistributionTransform(Uniform, source_dist)\nDistributionTransform(Normal, source_dist)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DistributionTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.LowRankAffineTransform","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BAT.LowRankAffineTransform <: BAT.AbstractAffineTransform\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAdaptive affine space transformation x = A * z + b with A a diagonal-plus-low-rank Gram factor (A * A' == D + W * S * W', represented as a MatrixShapedOperators Woodbury operator factor): a diagonal base geometry plus a correction along the directions where a diagonal geometry is insufficient. Tuning selects those directions by an eigenvalue cutoff (see FisherTransformTuning), which regularizes the geometry estimate compared to a full triangular matrix.\n\nApplying the transformation costs O(rank * n_dims). Dynamic fitting uses a thin basis. Initialization may use a dense decomposition governed by max_rank.\n\nDynamic Fisher tuning can make at most one rank-one correction attempt when cutoff >= 1.5, the dimension is at most 32, and a tuning cycle has enough steps; otherwise it tunes only the diagonal base. It fits from a fixed window and uses a guard followed by held-out validation. HMC keeps the diagonal kernel during both. MALA installs the candidate provisionally during its guard so it can retune and mix, then keeps it after acceptance or restores the diagonal transform after rejection. The correction must beat both the frozen diagonal base and its own diagonal projection. Each paired Fisher-loss comparison needs a positive one-sided 99% normal lower bound with at least 20 effective observations. This rejects purely diagonal updates without restricting the shape of a correlation direction.\n\nThis is a conservative held-out heuristic, not a finite-sample error-rate guarantee. Its asymptotic interpretation assumes stationary, mixing validation chains, finite long-run paired-loss variance, and independent walkers. Heavy-tailed cases outside those assumptions have only empirical evidence.\n\nConstructors:\n\nLowRankAffineTransform(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ninit::BAT.AbstractTransformInit: Transform initialization algorithm. Default: PriorApproxTransformInit()\nmax_rank::Int64: Maximum rank of the non-diagonal correction during initialization. 0     means no explicit cap. Dynamic Fisher tuning currently attempts one     rank-one correction. Default: 0\ncutoff::Float64: Relative eigenvalue cutoff used during initialization. Dynamic     Fisher tuning requires cutoff >= 1.5 and uses its fixed validated     rank-one policy. Default: 1.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.LowRankAffineTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.PathfinderTransformInit","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BAT.PathfinderTransformInit <: BAT.AbstractTransformInit\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nInitializes affine space transformations from local Gaussian target approximations obtained by running the Pathfinder algorithm (see BAT.pathfinder_gaussian_fit) from each initial walker position.\n\nRequires the BATContext to include an ADSelector and a gradient-based optimization backend: by default the Optim package must be loaded, alternatively set optalg explicitly.\n\nSee L. Zhang, B. Carpenter, A. Gelman and A. Vehtari, \"Pathfinder: Parallel quasi-Newton variational inference\", JMLR 23(306) (2022).\n\nConstructors:\n\nPathfinderTransformInit(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\noptalg::Any: Density maximization backend that generates the L-BFGS trajectory,     must record iterates and gradients (see maximize_density). Default: defaultpathfinder_optalg()\nhistory_length::Int64: L-BFGS history length of the inverse-Hessian estimates. Default: 6\nndraws_elbo::Int64: Number of Monte Carlo draws used to estimate the ELBO. Default: 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PathfinderTransformInit"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.enable_error_log","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.enable_error_log(enable::Bool = true)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nEnable/disable BAT's error (exception) log.\n\nThe error log is disabled by default.\n\nSee BAT.error_log.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.enable_error_log"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.error_log","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.error_log()\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nGet a log of certain exceptions throws by BAT, e.g. density evaluation errors.\n\nThe error log is disabled by default, use BAT.enable_error_log to enable it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.error_log"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.EvalException","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct EvalException <: Exception\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nConstructors:\n\nEvalException(func::Function, measure::Any, v::Any, ret::Any)\n\nThe target measure may be a measure or a generic density. The fields retain the supplied objects without copying them.\n\nFields:\n\nfunc::Function: Density evaluation function that failed.\nmeasure::Any: Measure or density being evaluated; the field retains its historical name.\nv::Any: Variate at which func(measure, v) failed.\nret::Any: Cause of failure: the invalid return value of func(measure, v), or the caught exception.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.EvalException"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.evalmeasure_impl","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.evalmeasure_impl(\n    em::EvaluatedMeasure,\n    algorithm,\n    context::BATContext\n)::EvaluatedMeasure\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nUsed internally by evalmeasure. Specialize BAT.evalmeasure_impl to implement new measure/distribution evaluation algorithms.\n\nImplementations receive the evaluation target as an EvaluatedMeasure and return an updated evaluated measure for the same underlying measure, so unevaluated(result) === unevaluated(em). The result is constructed via EvaluatedMeasure(em; ...); implementations decide themselves which entries of em to overwrite and which to only fill if absent, and they must record their evaluation by setting evalinfo = MeasureEvalInfo(algorithm, ...). Implementations that produce transformed-space content pass their transform_intent and f_transform in the same update, and report sample pairs stamped with the hash of that transformation (see BAT.BispacedMeasure).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.evalmeasure_impl"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.ext_default","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.ext_default(::PackageExtension{SomePackage}, ::Val{:SomeLabel}, args; kwargs...)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturns the default value selected by :SomeLabel within the context of the package extension that depends on SomePackage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ext_default"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.get_adselector","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.get_adselector(context::BATContext)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nReturns the automatic differentiation selector specified in context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.get_adselector"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.get_valid_adselector","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.get_valid_adselector(context::BATContext, algorithm)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nReturns the automatic differentiation selector specified in context, to be used for algorithm.\n\nThrows an exception if context specifies AutoDiffOperators.NoAutoDiff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.get_valid_adselector"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.PackageExtension","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type PackageExtension{pkgname}\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nRepresents a package extension that requires the package pkgname to be loaded.\n\nDo not construct instances of PackageExtension directly, use pkgext(:pkgname) instead which will check that the required extension is active.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PackageExtension"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.pkgext","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.pkgext(:SomePackage)::PackageExtension\nBAT.pkgext(Val(:SomePackage))::PackageExtension\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturns the PackageExtension instance that depends on the package SomePackage. Will throw an error if the extension is not active (because SomePackage` hasn't been loaded).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.pkgext"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.set_rng","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.set_rng(context::BATContext, rng::AbstractRNG)::BATContext\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nReturns a copy of context with the random number generator set to rng.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.set_rng"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.batmeasure","page":"Experimental Features","text":"batmeasure(obj)\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nConvert a measure-like obj to a measure that is compatible with BAT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.batmeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.BridgeSampling","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BridgeSampling <: IntegrationAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nBridgeSampling integration algorithm.\n\nSee X.-L. Meng and W. H. Wong, \"Simulating ratios of normalizing constants via a simple identity: a theoretical exploration\" (1996).\n\nConstructors:\n\nBridgeSampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: NormalBased()\nessalg::EffSampleSizeAlgorithm: Default: EffSampleSizeFromAC()\nstrict::Bool: Default: true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BridgeSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.EllipsoidalNestedSampling","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct EllipsoidalNestedSampling <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nUses the julia package NestedSamplers.jl to use nested sampling algorithm.\n\nSee J. Skilling, \"Nested sampling for general Bayesian computation\" (2006).\n\nConstructors:\n\nEllipsoidalNestedSampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: begin     pkgext(Val(:NestedSamplers))     #= /home/runner/work/BAT.jl/BAT.jl/src/extdefs/nestedsamplers_defs.jl:163 =#     UniformBased() end\nnum_live_points::Int64: Number of live-points. Default: 1000\nbound::BAT.ENSBound: Volume around the live-points. Default: ENSEllipsoidBound()\nproposal::BAT.ENSProposal: Algorithm used to choose new live-points. Default: ENSAutoProposal()\nenlarge::Float64: Scale factor for the volume. Default: 1.25\nmin_ncall::Int64: Number of iterations before the first bound will be fit. Default: 2numlivepoints\nmin_eff::Float64: Efficiency before fitting the first bound. Default: 0.1\ndlogz::Float64: Default: 0.01\nmax_iters::Any: Default: Inf\nmax_ncalls::Any: Default: 10 ^ 7\nmaxlogl::Any: Default: Inf\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the NestedSamplers.jl package  is loaded (e.g. via import).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.EllipsoidalNestedSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.EllipticalSliceMCMCSampling","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct EllipticalSliceMCMCSampling <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nSample a posterior with EllipticalSliceSampling.jl using a direct Gaussian prior transform. The sampler draws a Gaussian vector to define an ellipse through the current state. Each update draws a threshold uniformly below the current likelihood. It draws angle proposals uniformly from a bracket spanning the ellipse. It shrinks the bracket after rejections until a proposal's likelihood exceeds the threshold.\n\nSee I. Murray, R. Adams and D. MacKay, \"Elliptical slice sampling\" (2010).\n\nThis functionality requires EllipticalSliceSampling.jl to be loaded.\n\nConstructors:\n\nEllipticalSliceMCMCSampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ninit::InitvalAlgorithm: Initial-value algorithm. Default: begin     pkgext(Val(:EllipticalSliceSampling))     #= /home/runner/work/BAT.jl/BAT.jl/src/extdefs/ellipticalslicesampling_defs.jl:30 =#     InitFromTarget() end\nnsamples::Int64: Number of retained samples. Default: 10 ^ 4\nn_burnin::Int64: Number of initial samples to discard. Default: 10 ^ 3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.EllipticalSliceMCMCSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.GridSampler","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct GridSampler <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nSample from equidistantly distributed points in each dimension.\n\nConstructors:\n\nGridSampler(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: UniformBased()\nppa::Int64: Default: 100\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.GridSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.HierarchicalDistribution","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct HierarchicalDistribution <: ContinuousDistribution\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nA hierarchical distribution, useful for hierarchical models/priors.\n\nConstructors:\n\nHierarchicalDistribution(f::Function, primary_dist::NamedTupleDist)\n\nwith a functon f that returns a ContinuousDistribution for any variate v drawn from primary_dist.\n\nExample:\n\nhd = HierarchicalDistribution(\n    v -> NamedTupleDist(\n        baz = fill(Normal(v.bar, v.foo), 3)\n    ),\n    NamedTupleDist(\n        foo = Exponential(3.5),\n        bar = Normal(2.0, 1.0)\n    )\n)\n\nvarshape(hd) == NamedTupleShape(\n    foo = ScalarShape{Real}(),\n    bar = ScalarShape{Real}(),\n    baz = ArrayShape{Real}(3)\n)\n\nv = rand(hd)\n\nnote: Note\nAll fields of HierarchicalDistribution are considered internal and subject to change without deprecation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.HierarchicalDistribution"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.PriorImportanceSampler","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct PriorImportanceSampler <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nImportance sampler using IID samples from the prior.\n\nConstructors:\n\nPriorImportanceSampler(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nnsamples::Int64: Default: 10 ^ 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PriorImportanceSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.ReactiveNestedSampling","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct ReactiveNestedSampling <: AbstractUltraNestAlgorithmReactiv\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nUltraNest reactive nested sampling algorithm with.\n\nUses the UltraNest Python package, via UltraNest.jl (and PythonCall).\n\nSee J. Buchner, \"UltraNest - a robust, general purpose Bayesian inference engine\" (2021).\n\nConstructors:\n\nReactiveNestedSampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: begin     pkgext(Val(:UltraNest))     #= /home/runner/work/BAT.jl/BAT.jl/src/extdefs/ultranest_defs.jl:37 =#     UniformBased() end\nnum_test_samples::Int64: Test transform and likelihood with this number of random points for errors first. Useful to catch bugs. Default: 2\ndraw_multiple::Bool: If efficiency goes down, dynamically draw more points from the region between ndrawmin and ndrawmax. If set to False, few points are sampled at once. Default: true\nnum_bootstraps::Int64: Number of logZ estimators and MLFriends region bootstrap rounds     (see J. Buchner, \"A statistical test for Nested Sampling     algorithms\" (2016)). Default: 30\nndraw_min::Int64: Minimum number of points to simultaneously propose. Increase this if your likelihood makes vectorization very cheap. Default: 128\nndraw_max::Int64: Maximum number of points to simultaneously propose. Increase this if your likelihood makes vectorization very cheap. Memory allocation may be slow for extremely high values. Default: 65536\nupdate_interval_volume_fraction::Float64: Update region when the volume shrunk by this amount. Default: 0.8\nlog_interval::Int64: Update stdout status line every log_interval iterations. Default: -1\nshow_status::Bool: Show integration progress as a status line. Default: true\nviz_callback::Union{Nothing, Function}: Callback function when region was rebuilt. Allows to show current state of the live points. Default: nothing\ndlogz::Float64: Target evidence uncertainty. This is the std between bootstrapped logz integrators. Default: 0.5\ndKL::Float64: Target posterior uncertainty. This is the Kullback-Leibler divergence in nat between bootstrapped integrators. Default: 0.5\nfrac_remain::Float64: Integrate until this fraction of the integral is left in the remainder. Set to a low number (1e-2 … 1e-5) to make sure peaks are discovered. Set to a higher number (0.5) if you know the posterior is simple. Default: 0.01\nLepsilon::Float64: Terminate when live point likelihoods are all the same, within Lepsilon tolerance. Increase this when your likelihood function is inaccurate, to avoid unnecessary search. Default: 0.001\nmin_ess::Int64: Target number of effective posterior samples. Default: 400\nmax_iters::Int64: maximum number of integration iterations. Default: -1\nmax_ncalls::Int64: Stop after this many likelihood evaluations. Default: -1\nmax_num_improvement_loops::Int64: The algorithm tries to assess iteratively where more samples are needed. This number limits the number of improvement loops. Default: -1\nmin_num_live_points::Int64: Minimum number of live points throughout the run. Default: 400\ncluster_num_live_points::Int64: Require at least this many live points per detected cluster. Default: 40\ninsertion_test_window::Float64: z-score used as a threshold for the insertion order test. Set to infinity to disable. Default: 10.0\ninsertion_test_zscore_threshold::Float64: Number of iterations after which the insertion order test is reset. Default: 2.0\nexecutor::Any: Executor for posterior evaluation. Default: SequentialExec()\n\nnote: Note\nThis functionality is only available when the UltraNest package is loaded (e.g. via import UltraNest).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ReactiveNestedSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.SliceMCMCSampling","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct SliceMCMCSampling <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nSample a transformed target with SliceSampling.jl. The default sampler visits coordinates in random order. Each update draws a threshold uniformly below the current density. It expands a coordinate interval and draws proposals uniformly within it. It shrinks the interval after rejections until a proposal's density exceeds the threshold.\n\nSee R. M. Neal, \"Slice sampling\" (2003).\n\nThis functionality requires SliceSampling.jl to be loaded.\n\nConstructors:\n\nSliceMCMCSampling(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Transform the target into an unconstrained vector space. Default: begin     pkgext(Val(:SliceSampling))     #= /home/runner/work/BAT.jl/BAT.jl/src/extdefs/slicesampling_defs.jl:30 =#     NormalBased() end\ninit::InitvalAlgorithm: Initial-value algorithm. Default: InitFromTarget()\nsampler::Any: SliceSampling.jl sampler. Default: ext_default(pkgext(Val(:SliceSampling)), Val(:SAMPLER))\nnsamples::Int64: Number of retained samples. Default: 10 ^ 4\nn_burnin::Int64: Number of initial samples to discard. Default: 10 ^ 3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SliceMCMCSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.SobolSampler","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct SobolSampler <: AbstractSamplingAlgorithm\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nSample from Sobol sequence. Also see Sobol.jl.\n\nSee I. M. Sobol', \"On the distribution of points in a cube and the approximate evaluation of integrals\" (1967).\n\nConstructors:\n\nSobolSampler(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\npretransform::TransformIntent: Default: UniformBased()\nnsamples::Int64: Default: 10 ^ 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SobolSampler"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.truncate_batmeasure","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.truncate_batmeasure(density::BATMeasure, bounds::AbstractArray{<:Interval})::BATMeasure\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nTruncate density to bounds, the resulting density will be effectively zero outside of those bounds. In contrast Distributions.truncated, truncate_batmeasure does not renormalize the density.\n\nRequires varshape(density) isa ArrayShape.\n\nOnly supports densities that are essentially products of univariate distributions, as well as posterior densities with such densities as priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.truncate_batmeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.ValueAndThreshold","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct ValueAndThreshold{name}\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nHolds a (target) value, a comparison function and a threshold.\n\nConstructor: ValueAndThreshold{name}(value, cmp_function, threshold)\n\nConverts to a Bool accoring to cmp_function(value, threshold)\n\nExample:\n\nconvert(Bool, ValueAndThreshold{:max_error}(3.4, <, 5.2)) == true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ValueAndThreshold"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.validate_evalmeasure","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.validate_evalmeasure(\n    em::EvaluatedMeasure;\n    context::BATContext = get_batcontext()\n)::EvaluatedMeasure\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nVerify the transformed-space-view contract of em by re-deriving the view from (em.transform_intent, unevaluated(em)) and comparing values, and spot-check the stored sample log-densities against the measure (and its transformed representation). NaN sample log-densities are admissible (they mark values lost in LADJ-less sample transport), and log-density checks are skipped where the measure in question can't be point-evaluated. Computationally expensive, intended for tests and debugging, not for performance-critical code. Throws an exception if em violates its contract (or if transformed-space content exists but no validation points can be derived to verify it), returns em otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.validate_evalmeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCChainState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"MCMCChainState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nState of a MCMC chain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCChainState"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCChainStateInfo","page":"Experimental Features","text":"MCMCChainStateInfo\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nInformation about the state of an MCMC chain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCChainStateInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCIterator","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type MCMCIterator end\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nRepresents the current state of an MCMC chain.\n\nnote: Note\nThe details of the MCMCIterator and MCMCAlgorithm API (see below) currently do not form part of the stable API and are subject to change without deprecation.\n\nTo implement a new MCMC algorithm, subtypes of both MCMCAlgorithm and MCMCIterator are required.\n\nThe following methods must be defined for subtypes of MCMCIterator (e.g. SomeMCMCIter<:MCMCIterator):\n\nBAT.getproposal(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::MCMCAlgorithm\n\nBAT.mcmc_target(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::BATMeasure\n\nBAT.get_context(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::BATContext\n\nBAT.mcmc_info(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::MCMCIteratorInfo\n\nBAT.nsteps(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::Int\n\nBAT.nsamples(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::Int\n\nBAT.current_sample(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::DensitySample\n\nBAT.sample_type(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::Type{<:DensitySample}\n\nBAT.get_samples!(samples::DensitySampleVector, chain::SomeMCMCIter, nonzero_weights::Bool)::typeof(samples)\n\nBAT.next_cycle!(chain::SomeMCMCIter)::SomeMCMCIter\n\nBAT.mcmc_step!!(\n    chain::SomeMCMCIter\n    callback::Function,\n)::nothing\n\nThe following methods are implemented by default:\n\ngetproposal(chain::MCMCIterator)\nmcmc_target(chain::MCMCIterator)\nDensitySampleVector(chain::MCMCIterator)\nmcmc_iterate!!(chain::MCMCIterator, ...)\nmcmc_iterate!!(chains::AbstractVector{<:MCMCIterator}, ...)\nisvalidchain(chain::MCMCIterator)\nisviablechain(chain::MCMCIterator)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCIterator"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCProposal","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type MCMCProposal\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for MCMC proposal algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCProposal"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCProposalState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type MCMCProposalState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for MCMC proposal algorithm states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCProposalState"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCProposalTunerState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type MCMCProposalTunerState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for MCMC tuning algorithm states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCProposalTunerState"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"MCMCState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nCarrier type for the states of an MCMC chain, and the states  of the tuning and tempering algorithms used for sampling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCState"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCTempering","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type MCMCTempering\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for MCMC tempering algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCTempering"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MCMCTransformTunerState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type MCMCTransformTunerState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for MCMC tuning algorithm states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCTransformTunerState"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.MeasureEvalInfo","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BAT.MeasureEvalInfo\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nProperties:\n\nalgorithm: The algorithm used to evaluate the measure.\nresult: Algorithm-specific evaluation result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MeasureEvalInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.PolarShellDistribution","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.PolarShellDistribution{T<:Real} <: Distributions.Distribution{Multivariate,Continuous}\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nA doughnut-like distribution in two dimensions, in [r, phi] polar coordinates.\n\nThe distribution results from transforming the radial component of a base distribution the transport from a standard normal to a given radial distribution.\n\nConstructor:\n\n    PolarShellDistribution(\n        base_dist = MvNormal(Diagonal([1,1])),\n        radial_dist = LogNormal(0, 1)\n    )\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PolarShellDistribution"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.SimpleMCMCProposalState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type SimpleMCMCProposalState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for the states of simple MCMC proposal algorithms, that are implemented in BAT.jl. This is used to treat more complicated algorithms -that may depend on external packages- differently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SimpleMCMCProposalState"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.TemperingState","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type TemperingState\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for MCMC tempering algorithm states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.TemperingState"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.bat_makie_plot","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_makie_plot(\n    samples::DensitySampleVector,\n    recipes::NamedTuple = (upper = QuantileHist2D, diagonal = Hist1D, lower = Hist2D),\n    vsel::Vector{<:Integer} = [1, 2, 3],\n    N_max::Integer = 3;\n    dark::Bool = false,\n    display::Bool = true,\n    trace_nsteps::Integer = 20,\n    support = nothing,\n    step_intervals = nothing\n)\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nReturn a Makie.Figure containing an interactive corner plot for samples, with recipe menus, statistics/trace overlays, variable selection and Apply/All range controls. Requires Makie. The BAT light or dark theme is scoped to figure construction; unrelated figures and the global theme are unchanged.\n\nBy default, explicitly display the same figure before returning it. Set display = false to defer that display and optional shader warmup; the figure and controls are still constructed. Displaying or saving requires a loaded rendering backend. This keyword does not suppress automatic frontend display of the return value; use a trailing semicolon for that. For examples and failure behavior, see Interactive static Makie figures.\n\nWithout step_intervals, the range selects stored rows per walker and preserves their weights. Explicit repetition timing enables elapsed-step ranges and traces; bare sample metadata and statistical weights, including ARP weights, do not certify dwell durations. See Sample ranges and repetition timing for the per-row interval format, validity requirements and exact range controls.\n\nsupport declares hard KDE bounds in original sample coordinates, as a measure/prior or a vector of per-dimension (lo, hi) pairs. Invalid bounds or positive-weight observations outside support raise an error rather than being clamped. See KDE support and credible regions for normalization, zero-weight handling, approximation and numerical limits; use a histogram when a population cannot be represented by the supported KDE calculation.\n\nCalling empty!(fig) releases the static figure's content and BAT control subscriptions. If construction or explicit display fails, BAT attempts to release its resources before rethrowing the original error. Ordinary caller-owned composition is documented under Ordinary Makie composition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_makie_plot"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.bat_theme","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_theme()::Makie.Theme\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nThe BAT light Makie theme used by the corner-plot figure.\n\nbat_makie_plot and the live visualizer apply it to their own figures only, via a scoped with_theme. To use it for every Makie plot in a session, apply it yourself with Makie.set_theme!(bat_theme()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_theme"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.bat_theme_dark","page":"Experimental Features","text":"bat_theme_dark()::Makie.Theme\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nThe BAT dark Makie theme used by the corner-plot figure. Scoped to BAT's own figures like bat_theme; apply it with Makie.set_theme! for a session-wide dark theme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bat_theme_dark"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.BATVisBackend","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type BATVisBackend\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract supertype of sampling-visualization backend configurations (e.g. BATMakieVisualization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATVisBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.BATMakieRecipe","page":"Experimental Features","text":"abstract type BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract supertype of the Makie corner-plot cell recipes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATMakieRecipe"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.BATMakieVisualization","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BATMakieVisualization <: BATVisBackend\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nConfigure a live Makie corner plot for MCMC sampling. Pass the configuration to BATVisualizer and then to BATContext(visualizer = vis). Creating the visualizer requires Makie; displaying it requires a rendering backend such as GLMakie. Create a fresh visualizer for each sampling run.\n\nConstructors:\n\nBATMakieVisualization(;\n    dark = false, max_buffered = 200, adaptive_batching = true,\n    batch_growth_rate = 1.2, trace_nsteps = 20, observer_timeout = 60.0\n)\n\nBATMakieVisualization(\n    recipes, vsel, N_max, n_batch, max_buffered, adaptive_batching,\n    batch_growth_rate, poll_interval, dark, triagonal_config, diagonal_config;\n    observer_timeout = 60.0\n)\n\nBATMakieVisualization(\n    recipes, vsel, N_max, n_batch, max_buffered, adaptive_batching,\n    batch_growth_rate, poll_interval, dark, triagonal_config, diagonal_config,\n    observer_timeout\n)\n\nThe keyword constructor accepts only the six keywords shown. It fixes vsel = [1, 2, 3], N_max = 3, n_batch = 50, poll_interval = 0.1 and the default recipes/configurations below. Use a positional constructor to supply those settings. trace_nsteps is an integer of at least one; it sets triagonal_config.trace_nsteps and is not a separate field.\n\nFields:\n\nrecipes::NamedTuple: initial (upper, diagonal, lower) recipe types. The keyword constructor uses (upper = QuantileHist2D, diagonal = Hist1D, lower = Hist2D).\nvsel::Vector{Int}: initial variable indices. Construction truncates a selection longer than N_max; startup removes indices outside the available coordinates. The initial selection is applied once. Default: [1, 2, 3].\nN_max::Int: fixed positive grid size. Default: 3.\nn_batch::Int: initial flush threshold in completed walker-steps across all chains. Default: 50.\nmax_buffered::Int: initial backpressure threshold in the same units. Require 0 < n_batch <= max_buffered; this is not a strict memory limit. Default: 200.\nadaptive_batching::Bool: whether the effective flush and backpressure thresholds grow after each flush. Default: true.\nbatch_growth_rate::Float64: finite growth factor greater than one, required even when adaptive batching is disabled. Default: 1.2.\npoll_interval::Float64: finite positive polling interval in seconds. Default: 0.1.\ndark::Bool: select the BAT dark theme for the live figure. Default: false.\ntriagonal_config::NamedTuple: off-diagonal recipe and overlay settings. The keyword constructor supplies BAT defaults, including trace_nsteps = 20 unless overridden, and a statistics color chosen for dark.\ndiagonal_config::NamedTuple: diagonal recipe and overlay settings. The keyword constructor supplies BAT defaults with a statistics color chosen for dark.\nobserver_timeout::Float64: finite positive timeout in seconds for each continuous backpressure wait and, separately, finalization. Default: 60.0.\n\nDefaults listed above are those of the keyword constructor. Positional values are converted to the stored field types and validated. Timeout expiry disables observation while sampling continues; it requires scheduler progress and cannot cancel a stuck native renderer. Inspect BAT.visualizer_status, and use BAT.dispose_visualizer! to release a retained completed view.\n\nSee Live Makie configuration for batching, timing and RNG guarantees, and Live Makie session lifetime for figure ownership and cleanup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATMakieVisualization"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.BATVisualizer","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct BATVisualizer{B<:BATVisBackend}\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nCarries a visualization backend configuration and, once sampling starts, the live visualization state. Pass via BATContext(visualizer = ...). A used visualizer is single-use – create a fresh one per sampling run.\n\nConstructors:\n\nBATVisualizer(): no visualization\nBATVisualizer(backend::BATVisBackend): e.g. BATVisualizer(BATMakieVisualization())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATVisualizer"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.visualizer_figure","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.visualizer_figure(vis::BATVisualizer)\n\nExperimental, non-exported API. Return the retained figure of a successfully completed visualization, or nothing when unavailable. Backends without a retained figure, including the no-op backend, return nothing.\n\nFor Makie, observation must be complete and its tasks settled, with a valid idle view and no pending disposal. Active, failed, disposed or busy sessions return nothing, as do calls reentered from view callbacks. This query neither waits for completion nor constructs, displays or renders a figure.\n\nCall on the thread that created the live view. A different thread raises ArgumentError when the session can be inspected; contention on its control guard takes precedence and returns nothing without waiting.\n\nThe result is a borrowed mutable figure, not a copy or ownership transfer. Coordinate subsequent Makie operations with callbacks and disposal on the owning thread. Later closing or dispose_visualizer! can empty that same figure; the query does not protect its later use. Inspect visualizer_status for lifecycle state and diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.visualizer_figure"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.visualizer_status","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.visualizer_status(vis::BATVisualizer)\n\nExperimental, non-exported API. Return a read-only lifecycle snapshot without accessing the renderer. phase, outcome and reason describe the run; data_complete and last_publication describe confirmed observation history. quiesced records actual run-resource completion, independently of cleanup_pending, view_disposed and presentation_valid. history_released records settled release of observation history, not garbage collection of external aliases. error records the first failure and its phase; cleanup_error is a secondary cleanup diagnostic. A no-op backend reports an inactive, quiesced session. Backends must not expose mutable internal storage through this snapshot.\n\nMakie retains bounded inert error summaries. Built-in concrete string messages are copied with a size limit; other errors retain their type/phase and fallback text. The query does not invoke custom exception formatting or retain task stacks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.visualizer_status"},{"category":"function","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.dispose_visualizer!","page":"Experimental Features","text":"BAT.dispose_visualizer!(vis::BATVisualizer)\n\nExperimental, non-exported API. Disable observation and dispose of resources owned by this visualization session. Repeated disposal is harmless. Disposal does not cancel sampling or affect other figures. A backend may report pending cleanup while an already admitted view operation is still running.\n\nMakie disposal releases the session's observation history and graph caches once cleanup settles, without requiring status polling. Successful completion keeps the interactive figure/history until disposal. visualizer_status reports history_released separately from historical completeness. Caller-held data, controls, configuration and externally retained diagnostics remain independent owners. Cleanup failures can be retried by disposing again on the owning thread. If the sampling owner has acquired the Makie session, disposal during numerical initialization lets that same acquisition continue without starting a view. The session remains single-use: disposal before acquisition, reuse, and startup from another task or thread still reject. Numerical and startup failures still propagate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.dispose_visualizer!"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.ChainScatter2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct ChainScatter2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D sample scatter colored by MCMC chain; only offered when the samples carry chain identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ChainScatter2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Cov2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Cov2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nStats overlay: covariance ellipse with principal axes (2D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Cov2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Errorbars1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Errorbars1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nMean with ±nσ error bars (1D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Errorbars1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Errorbars2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Errorbars2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nMean with ±nσ error bars in both variables (2D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Errorbars2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Hexbin2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Hexbin2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D hexagonal binning colored by absolute weighted counts (off-diagonal cells). Thresholds use the same count units. Displayed bins must retain their normalized color positions through Makie's Float32 conversion; unsupported magnitudes or insufficient color resolution raise an error. Dispose and recreate a caller-owned figure after a rejected update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Hexbin2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Hist1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Hist1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n1D marginal histogram (diagonal cells).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Hist1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Hist2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Hist2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D marginal histogram heatmap (off-diagonal cells).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Hist2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.KDE1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct KDE1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n1D kernel density estimate (diagonal cells), represented by a piecewise-linear curve. With declared hard support, the returned curve stays inside support and has unit trapezoid integral. Boundary correction uses finite Gaussian reflections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.KDE1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.KDE2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct KDE2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D kernel density heatmap (off-diagonal cells), represented by constant-density cells with explicit edges. With declared hard support, complete cells stay inside support and their density times area sums to one before the visibility mask. Makie stores numeric heatmap colors as Float32; a visible density that would overflow or become zero raises an error. Change coordinate units or use QuantileKDE2D, whose colors represent credible regions directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.KDE2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Mean1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Mean1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nStats overlay: dashed mean line (1D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Mean1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Mean2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Mean2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nStats overlay: mean crosshair (2D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Mean2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.PDF1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct PDF1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nNormal (Gaussian) fit to the 1D marginal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PDF1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.QuantileHist1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct QuantileHist1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n1D histogram with whole bins colored by highest-density credible region. Cells are ranked by density; coverage integrates their actual widths. Complete threshold plateaus are included, so achieved probability can exceed the request. This is a whole-bin approximation, not a minimum-width subset optimization. Zero is an inactive level sentinel; up to three positive levels in (0, 1] are supported, including full-mass regions at one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.QuantileHist1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.QuantileHist2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct QuantileHist2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D histogram with whole cells colored by highest-density credible region. Cells are ranked by density and accumulated by actual cell probability. Complete threshold plateaus are included. Original cell edges determine the colored area. See QuantileHist1D for level and whole-bin approximation conventions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.QuantileHist2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.QuantileKDE1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct QuantileKDE1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n1D KDE with highest-density credible regions. Region contents integrate the piecewise-linear curve, including interpolated threshold crossings. Separated modes can produce disconnected regions; complete tied plateaus are included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.QuantileKDE1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.QuantileKDE2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct QuantileKDE2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D KDE with highest-density credible regions. Cells are ranked by density and their probabilities use actual cell areas. Complete threshold ties are included, so achieved content may exceed the requested content, including for a single cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.QuantileKDE2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Scatter2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Scatter2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\n2D sample scatter (off-diagonal cells).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Scatter2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Std1D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Std1D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nStats overlay: ±nσ standard-deviation lines (1D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Std1D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Std2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Std2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nStats overlay: ±nσ standard-deviation cross lines (2D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Std2D"},{"category":"type","location":"experimental_api/#BAT.Trace2D","page":"Experimental Features","text":"struct Trace2D <: BATMakieRecipe\n\nExperimental feature, not yet part of stable public API.\n\nRecency-colored trace of each walker's last trace_nsteps steps in the latest cycle contributing to the selected window. Paths are split at gaps and never connect different cycles or walkers. An always-live overlay (show_trace_upper/show_trace_lower), never in the recipe dropdown; requires captured repetition timing or explicit step_intervals for static plots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.Trace2D"},{"category":"section","location":"internal_api/#Internal-API","page":"Internal API","text":"note: Note\nThis is the documentation of BAT's internal API. The internal API is fully accessible to users, but all aspects of it are subject to change without deprecation. Functionalities of the internal API that, over time, turn out to require user access (e.g. to support advanced use cases) will be evolved to gain a stable interface and then promoted to the public API.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"internal_api/#Types","page":"Internal API","text":"Pages = [\"internal_api.md\"]\nOrder = [:type]","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"internal_api/#Functions-and-macros","page":"Internal API","text":"Pages = [\"internal_api.md\"]\nOrder = [:macro, :function]","title":"Functions and macros"},{"category":"section","location":"internal_api/#Documentation","page":"Internal API","text":"","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.AbstractAffineTransform","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type BAT.AbstractAffineTransform <: BAT.AbstractAdaptiveTransform\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nSupertype for adaptive affine space transformations x = A * z + b, which differ in the structure they impose on A.\n\nImplementation\n\nSubtypes must have a field init holding a BAT.AbstractTransformInit algorithm, and must specialize BAT._affine_init_A to build A in their structure from an approximate covariance. Transform tunings maintain that structure across updates and specialize on the concrete type in turn (see BAT._fisher_estimator for FisherTransformTuning).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractAffineTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.AbstractSampleGenerator","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type AbstractSampleGenerator\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract super type for sample generators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractSampleGenerator"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BasicMvStatistics","page":"Internal API","text":"BasicMvStatistics{T<:Real,W}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nW must either be Weights (no bias correction) or one of AnalyticWeights, FrequencyWeights or ProbabilityWeights to specify the desired bias correction method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BasicMvStatistics"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BATMeasure","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type BATMeasure <:AbstractMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nSubtypes must implement DensityInterface.logdensityof and ValueShapes.varshape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BATPushFwdMeasure","page":"Internal API","text":"BATPushFwdMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATPushFwdMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BATPwrMeasure","page":"Internal API","text":"BATPwrMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATPwrMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BATSuperpositionMeasure","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BATSuperpositionMeasure <: BATMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nSuperposition (sum) of measures.\n\nExtended help\n\nThe log-density of the superposition is computed directly via logaddexp over the component log-densities in an autodiff-friendly way.\n\nSuperposing equal BAT measures does not collapse them, for type stability.\n\nMeasureBase.superpose generates a BATSuperpositionMeasure if its first argument is a BATMeasure, all other arguments must then be a BATMeasure as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATSuperpositionMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BATWeightedMeasure","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BATWeightedMeasure <: BATMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BATWeightedMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.CholeskyPartialWhitening","page":"Internal API","text":"struct CholeskyPartialWhitening <: WhiteningAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nWhitening transformation based on a Cholesky transformation of the empirical sample covariance matrix.\n\nOnly transforms dimensions (degrees of freedom) for which the marginalized distribution asymptotically approaches zero in the positive and negative direction.\n\nConstructors:\n\nCholeskyPartialWhitening()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.CholeskyPartialWhitening"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.CholeskyWhitening","page":"Internal API","text":"struct CholeskyWhitening <: WhiteningAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nWhitening transformation based on a Cholesky transformation of the empirical sample covariance matrix.\n\nSee A. Kessy, A. Lewin and K. Strimmer, \"Optimal Whitening and Decorrelation\" (2018).\n\nConstructors:\n\nCholeskyWhitening()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.CholeskyWhitening"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSAutoProposal","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSAutoProposal <: ENSProposal\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nChoose the proposal depending from the number of dimensions:      ndims < 10: Proposals.Uniform,       10 ≤ ndims ≤ 20: Proposals.RWalk,       ndims > 20: Proposals.Slice\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSAutoProposal"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSBound","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type ENSBound\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for the bounds of the sampling region used by EllipsoidalNestedSampling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSBound"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSEllipsoidBound","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSEllipsoidBound <: ENSBound\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nEllipsoid bound means that a n-dimensional ellipsoid limits the sampling volume.\n\nSee P. Mukherjee, D. Parkinson and A. R. Liddle, \"A Nested Sampling Algorithm for Cosmological Model Selection\" (2006).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSEllipsoidBound"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSMultiEllipsoidBound","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSMultiEllipsoidBound <: ENSBound\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nMulti ellipsoid bound means that there are multiple elliposid in an optimal clustering are used to limit the sampling volume.\n\nSee F. Feroz, M. P. Hobson and M. Bridges, \"MultiNest: an efficient and robust Bayesian inference tool for cosmology and particle physics\" (2009).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSMultiEllipsoidBound"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSNoBounds","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSNoBounds <: ENSBound\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nNo bounds means that the whole volume from the unit Cube is used to find new points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSNoBounds"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSProposal","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type ENSProposal\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for the algorithms to propose new live points used used by EllipsoidalNestedSampling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSProposal"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSRandomWalk","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSRandomWalk <: ENSProposal\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nNew live point is proposed by using a random walk away from an existing live point.\n\nConstructors:\n\nENSRandomWalk(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nratio::Float64: Acceptance ratio for the random walk. Default: 0.5\nwalks::Int64: Minimum number of random walk steps. Default: 25\nscale::Float64: Scale of the proposal distribution. Default: 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSRandomWalk"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSSlice","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSSlice <: ENSProposal\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nNew live point is proposed by a serie of random slices from an existing live-point.\n\nSee R. M. Neal, \"Slice sampling\" (2003).\n\nConstructors:\n\nENSSlice(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\nslices::Int64: Minimum number of slices Default: 5\nscale::Float64: Scale of the proposal distribution. Default: 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSSlice"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.ENSUniformly","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.ENSUniformly <: ENSProposal\n\nExperimental feature, not part of stable public API.\n\nEach point in the bounding volume has an uniform chance to be proposed as a new live point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.ENSUniformly"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.FullMeasureTransform","page":"Internal API","text":"struct FullMeasureTransform <: TransformAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nTransform the density as a whole a given specified target space. Operations that use the gradient of the density will require to the log(abs(jacobian)) of the transformation to be auto-differentiable.\n\nConstructors:\n\nFullMeasureTransform()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.FullMeasureTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.JointLikelihood","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.JointLikelihood <: MeasureBase.AbstractLikelihood\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nCombines several likelihoods that share a common parameter space.\n\nUser code should not instantiate JointLikelihood directly, but use joint_likelihood instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.JointLikelihood"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.LFDensity","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.LFDensity{F}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nWraps a log-density function log_f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.LFDensity"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.AbstractTransformInit","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type BAT.AbstractTransformInit\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for algorithms that initialize adaptive MCMC space transformations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.AbstractTransformInit"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.LFDensityWithGrad","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.LFDensityWithGrad{F,G} <: BATDensity\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nConstructors:\n\nLFDensityWithGrad(logf, valgradlogf)\n\nA density defined by a function that computes it's logarithmic value at given points, as well as a function that computes both the value and the gradient.\n\nIt must be safe to execute both functions in parallel on multiple threads and processes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.LFDensityWithGrad"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.LogDVal","page":"Internal API","text":"struct LogDVal{T<:Real}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nLogDVal is deprecated and will be removed in future major or even minor BAT versions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.LogDVal"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.MCMCSampleGenerator","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.MCMCSampleGenerator\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nMCMC sample generator, holds the (mutable) states of the MCMC chains. Consumers must not mutate the chain states, continuing sample generation requires a deep copy.\n\nConstructors:\n\nMCMCSampleGenerator(mc_state::AbstractVector{<:MCMCIterator})\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCSampleGenerator"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.MCMCStepInfo","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.MCMCStepInfo\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nPer-walker information about one MCMC transition, produced by mcmc_propose!! and consumed by the tuning machinery and sample weighting. p_accept is always present; gradient-based proposals additionally provide the z-space log-density gradients at the selected states (z_grads) and trajectory diagnostics (divergent, tree_depth, n_leapfrog), which are nothing for proposals that don't compute them. walker_order indexes these vectors in logical-walker order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MCMCStepInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.MeasureLike","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.MeasureLike = Union{...}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nUnion of all types that BAT will accept as a measures or convert to measures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.MeasureLike"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.NoWhitening","page":"Internal API","text":"struct NoWhitening <: WhiteningAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nNo-op whitening transformation, leaves samples unchanged.\n\nConstructors:\n\nNoWhitening()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.NoWhitening"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.OnlineMvCov","page":"Internal API","text":"OnlineMvCov{T<:AbstractFloat,W} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nImplementation based on the variance calculation algorithms of B. P. Welford (1962) and D. H. D. West (1979).\n\nW must either be Weights (no bias correction) or one of AnalyticWeights, FrequencyWeights or ProbabilityWeights to specify the desired bias correction method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.OnlineMvCov"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.OnlineMvMean","page":"Internal API","text":"OnlineMvMean{T<:AbstractFloat} <: AbstractVector{T}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nMultivariate mean implemented via Kahan-Babuška-Neumaier summation (A. Neumaier (1974)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.OnlineMvMean"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.OnlineUvMean","page":"Internal API","text":"OnlineUvMean{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nUnivariate mean implemented via Kahan-Babuška-Neumaier summation (A. Neumaier (1974)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.OnlineUvMean"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.OnlineUvVar","page":"Internal API","text":"OnlineUvVar{T<:AbstractFloat,W}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nImplementation based on the variance calculation algorithms of B. P. Welford (1962) and D. H. D. West (1979).\n\nW must either be Weights (no bias correction) or one of AnalyticWeights, FrequencyWeights or ProbabilityWeights to specify the desired bias correction method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.OnlineUvVar"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.PriorApproxTransformInit","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.PriorApproxTransformInit <: BAT.AbstractTransformInit\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nInitializes affine space transformations from the approximate covariance and mean of the prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.PriorApproxTransformInit"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.SampleTransformation","page":"Internal API","text":"struct SampleTransformation <: TransformAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.SampleTransformation"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StanLikeTuning","page":"Internal API","text":"struct StanLikeTuning <: MCMCTransformTuning\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nTunes MCMC space transformations from sample covariance estimates, accumulated over Stan-like adaptation windows of doubling size (see the Stan HMC parameters documentation).\n\nEach explicit tuning initialization or reinitialization clears the window statistics, retaining the learned transformation. Iterator chunks and chain cycle changes alone do not restart adaptation; initialization output does not seed this tuner. Each window uses the kept post-step states with unit weights.\n\nAt initialization, buffers must be nonnegative, the initial window width positive, and the nonnegative step horizon representable as Int. Horizons zero and one are inactive. The schedule retains BAT's one-step margin and omits a split at the last adaptation step; only windows with retained splits collect observations. After the final split, the internal counter stops.\n\nA window with fewer than two kept states leaves the transformation unchanged. Otherwise, finite covariance estimates use the existing positive-factorization policy, including for singular covariance. Nonfinite covariance or a nonfinite/singular converted factor raises an error before installing new geometry. Such an error need not roll back accumulated statistics or the step index; reinitialize the tuner before reuse.\n\nConstructors:\n\nStanLikeTuning(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ninit_buffer::Int64: width of initial fast adaptation interval Default: 75\nterm_buffer::Int64: width of final fast adaptation interval Default: 50\nwindow_size::Int64: initial width of slow adaptation interval Default: 25\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StanLikeTuning"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StandardMvNormal","page":"Internal API","text":"StandardMvNormal{T<:Real} <: Distributions.AbstractMvNormal\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nA standard n-dimensional multivariate normal distribution with it's mean at the origin and an identity covariance matrix.\n\nConstructor:\n\n    StandardMvNormal(n::Integer)\n    StandardMvNormal{T<:Real}(n::Integer)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StandardMvNormal"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StandardMvUniform","page":"Internal API","text":"StandardMvUniform{T<:Real} <: Distributions.Distribution{Multivariate,Continuous}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nA standard n-dimensional multivariate uniform distribution, from zero to one in each dimension.\n\nConstructor:\n\n    StandardMvUniform(n::Integer)\n    StandardMvUniform{T<:Real}(n::Integer)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StandardMvUniform"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StandardUvNormal","page":"Internal API","text":"StandardUvNormal{T<:Real} <: Distributions.Distribution{Univariate,Continuous}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nA standard normal distribution with a mean of zero and a variance of one.\n\nConstructor:\n\n    StandardUvNormal()\n    StandardUvNormal{T<:Real}()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StandardUvNormal"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StandardUvUniform","page":"Internal API","text":"StandardUvUniform{T<:Real} <: Distributions.Distribution{Univariate,Continuous}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nA standard uniform distribution between zero and one.\n\nConstructor:\n\n    StandardUvUniform()\n    StandardUvUniform{T<:Real}()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StandardUvUniform"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StatisticalWhitening","page":"Internal API","text":"struct StatisticalWhitening <: WhiteningAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nWhitening transformation based statistical whitening.\n\nSee A. Kessy, A. Lewin and K. Strimmer, \"Optimal Whitening and Decorrelation\" (2018).\n\nConstructors:\n\nStatisticalWhitening()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StatisticalWhitening"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.StepSizeAdaptor","page":"Internal API","text":"struct StepSizeAdaptor <: BAT.HMCTuning\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nTunes the scalar proposal scale of gradient-based MCMC proposals via Nesterov dual averaging, targeting the proposal's target acceptance rate: the leapfrog step size of HamiltonianMC and the Langevin step scale of MALAProposal.\n\nSee M. D. Hoffman and A. Gelman, \"The No-U-Turn Sampler: Adaptively Setting Path Lengths in Hamiltonian Monte Carlo\" (2014), section 3.2.\n\nInvalid adaptation controls are rejected with ArgumentError when the proposal tuner state is constructed, before sampling begins. An update or finalization that cannot represent a finite positive scale raises ArgumentError without installing an invalid scale.\n\nConstructors:\n\nStepSizeAdaptor(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ngamma::Real: Positive finite adaptation regularization scale. Default: 0.05\nt0::Real: Finite non-negative adaptation iteration offset. Default: 10.0\nkappa::Real: Finite adaptation relaxation exponent in (0.5, 1]. Default: 0.75\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.StepSizeAdaptor"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.DiagonalAffineTransform","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.DiagonalAffineTransform <: BAT.AbstractAffineTransform\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nAdaptive affine space transformation x = A * z + b with a diagonal matrix A, initialized via an BAT.AbstractTransformInit algorithm. Cheap to apply and to tune, but blind to correlations.\n\nConstructors:\n\nDiagonalAffineTransform(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ninit::BAT.AbstractTransformInit: Transform initialization algorithm. Default: PriorApproxTransformInit()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.DiagonalAffineTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.TriangularAffineTransform","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.TriangularAffineTransform <: BAT.AbstractAffineTransform\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nAdaptive affine space transformation x = A * z + b with a lower-triangular matrix A, initialized via an BAT.AbstractTransformInit algorithm.\n\nConstructors:\n\nTriangularAffineTransform(; fields...)\n\nFields:\n\ninit::BAT.AbstractTransformInit: Transform initialization algorithm. Default: PriorApproxTransformInit()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.TriangularAffineTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.UnitTransformInit","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BAT.UnitTransformInit <: BAT.AbstractTransformInit\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nInitializes affine space transformations to the identity map. The natural initialization for inner components of an AdaptiveTransformChain, where the outer components already carry the target geometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.UnitTransformInit"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.UnshapeTransformation","page":"Internal API","text":"struct UnshapeTransformation <: TransformAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.UnshapeTransformation"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.WhiteningAlgorithm","page":"Internal API","text":"abstract type WhiteningAlgorithm\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nAbstract type for sample whitening algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.WhiteningAlgorithm"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.argchoice_msg","page":"Internal API","text":"argchoice_msg(f::Base.Callable, argname::Val, x)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nGenerates an information message regarding the choice of value x for argument argname of function f.\n\nThe value x will often be the result of bat_default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.argchoice_msg"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.bg_R_2sqr","page":"Internal API","text":"bg_R_2sqr(stats::AbstractVector{<:MCMCBasicStats}; corrected::Bool = false)\nbg_R_2sqr(samples::AbstractVector{<:DensitySampleVector}; corrected::Bool = false)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nBrooks-Gelman R_2^2 for all DOF. If normality is assumed, 'corrected' should be set to true to account for the sampling variability.\n\nSee S. P. Brooks and A. Gelman, \"General Methods for Monitoring Convergence of Iterative Simulations\" (1998); the corrected option implements the paper's df-corrected variant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.bg_R_2sqr"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.checked_logdensityof","page":"Internal API","text":"checked_logdensityof(target, v)\nchecked_logdensityof(target)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nEvaluates a measure or density via DensityInterface.logdensityof and performs additional checks. The one-argument form returns a function of the variate.\n\nThe generic method preserves valid Real results, including negative infinity, without conversion. It throws a BAT.EvalException on any of these conditions:\n\nDensityInterface.logdensityof throws an exception.\nThe return value of DensityInterface.logdensityof is NaN.\nThe return value of DensityInterface.logdensityof is an equivalent of positive infinity.\n\nThe Zygote rule checks variate shape before evaluation when varshape has a uniquely applicable method for the target. Absent or ambiguous shape metadata does not impose a shape preflight. Errors from an applicable metadata method or the shape check are not wrapped. Errors raised later by the reverse pullback are also not wrapped. Specialized measure methods may impose different checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.checked_logdensityof"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.dist_samples_mean_zscores","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.dist_samples_mean_zscores(\n    dist::Distribution, smpls::DensitySampleVector,\n    context::BATContext = get_batcontext()\n)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nZ-scores of the sample means of smpls against the true means of dist, based on Monte Carlo standard errors derived from the effective sample size.\n\nRequires mean and var to be defined for unshaped(dist).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.dist_samples_mean_zscores"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.drop_low_weight_samples","page":"Internal API","text":"drop_low_weight_samples(\n    samples::DensitySampleVector,\n    fraction::Real = 10^-4\n)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nDrop fraction of the total probability mass from samples to filter out the samples with the lowest weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.drop_low_weight_samples"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.fft_autocor","page":"Internal API","text":"fft_autocor(v::AbstractVector{<:Real})\nfft_autocor(v::AbstractVectorOfSimilarVectors{<:Real})\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nCompute the autocorrelation function (ACF) of variate series v, separately for each degree of freedom.\n\nUses FFT, in contract to StatsBase.autocor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.fft_autocor"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.fft_autocov","page":"Internal API","text":"fft_autocov(v::AbstractVector{<:Real})\nfft_autocov(v::AbstractVectorOfSimilarVectors{<:Real})\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nCompute the autocovariance of of variate series v, separately for each degree of freedom.\n\nUses FFT, in contract to StatsBase.autocov.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.fft_autocov"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.find_marginalmodes","page":"Internal API","text":"find_marginalmodes(marg::MarginalDist)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nFind the modes of a MarginalDist. Returns a vector of the bin-centers of the bin(s) with the heighest weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.find_marginalmodes"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.get_bin_centers","page":"Internal API","text":"get_bin_centers(marg::MarginalDist)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturns a vector of the bin-centers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.get_bin_centers"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.get_iid_sampleable_approx","page":"Internal API","text":"get_iid_sampleable_approx(target)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nObtain a measure from the target that can be sampled to obtain iid samples for a MCMCGlobalProposal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.get_iid_sampleable_approx"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.getlikelihood","page":"Internal API","text":"getlikelihood(posterior::AbstractPosteriorMeasure)::BATDensity\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nThe likelihood density of posterior. The likelihood may or may not be normalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.getlikelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.getprior","page":"Internal API","text":"getprior(posterior::AbstractPosteriorMeasure)::BATMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nThe prior density of posterior. The prior may or may not be normalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.getprior"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.gr_Rsqr","page":"Internal API","text":"gr_Rsqr(stats::AbstractVector{<:MCMCBasicStats})\ngr_Rsqr(samples::AbstractVector{<:DensitySampleVector})\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nGelman-Rubin R^2 for all DOF.\n\nSee A. Gelman and D. B. Rubin, \"Inference from Iterative Simulation Using Multiple Sequences\" (1992).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.gr_Rsqr"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.has_uhc_support","page":"Internal API","text":"has_uhc_support(m)::Bool\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nIs the support of measure m limited to the unit hypercube?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.has_uhc_support"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.hmc_find_good_stepsize","page":"Internal API","text":"hmc_find_good_stepsize(\n    rng::AbstractRNG, f_logdgrad::Function, q0::AbstractVector{<:Real};\n    init_stepsize::Real = 0.1, max_niters::Integer = 100\n)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nHeuristically search a leapfrog step size at position q0 for which the single-step Metropolis acceptance ratio lies between 1/4 and 3/4 (see M. D. Hoffman and A. Gelman, \"The No-U-Turn Sampler: Adaptively Setting Path Lengths in Hamiltonian Monte Carlo\" (2014), algorithm 4).\n\nGiven a vector of positions instead of a single position, probes every position and returns the smallest step size found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.hmc_find_good_stepsize"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.hmc_nuts_transition","page":"Internal API","text":"hmc_nuts_transition(\n    rng::AbstractRNG, f_logdgrad::Function, z0::HMCPhasePoint,\n    stepsize::Real, max_depth::Integer, max_delta_energy::Real\n)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nPerform a single multinomial NUTS transition from phase point z0, doubling the trajectory until the generalized no-U-turn condition triggers, the energy error exceeds max_delta_energy or the tree depth reaches max_depth.\n\nf_logdgrad(q) must return the tuple (logd, grad) of the target log-density and its gradient.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple with the fields z (the sampled phase point), p_accept (average leapfrog Metropolis acceptance probability), depth, n_leapfrog and divergent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.hmc_nuts_transition"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.is_log_zero","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.is_log_zero(x::Real, T::Type = typeof(x)}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nCheck if x is an equivalent of log of zero, resp. negative infinity, in respect to type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.is_log_zero"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.issymmetric_around_origin","page":"Internal API","text":"issymmetric_around_origin(d::Distribution)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturns true (resp. false) if the Distribution is symmetric (resp. non-symmetric) around the origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.issymmetric_around_origin"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.log_zero_density","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.log_zero_density(T::Type{<:Real})\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nlog-density value to assume for regions of implicit zero density, e.g. outside of variate/parameter bounds/support.\n\nReturns an equivalent of negative infinity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.log_zero_density"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.logvalof","page":"Internal API","text":"logvalof(r::NamedTuple{(...,:log,...)})::Real\nlogvalof(r::LogDVal)::Real\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.logvalof"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.maximize_density","page":"Internal API","text":"maximize_density(f_logdensity, x_init::AbstractVector{<:Real}, algorithm, context::BATContext)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nMaximize the log-density function f_logdensity, starting at x_init, using optimization algorithm.\n\nThe user is responsible for shaping flogdensity in a way that works well with the algorithm (typically by transforming it to an unbounded space), `maximizedensity` does not apply automatic space transformations.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple (result = x_optimal, trace = ..., info = ...) with the optimum in the same space, an optional optimization trace and algorithm-specific optimizer information.\n\ntrace is nothing unless trace recording was requested via the algorithm (field store_trace of OptimAlg and OptimizationAlg). When present, it is a NamedTuple of iteration-indexed vectors: v (the iterates) and, depending on backend and optimizer, logd (the log-density values at the iterates) and grad_logd (the log-density gradients at the iterates).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.maximize_density"},{"category":"type","location":"internal_api/#BAT.BispacedMeasure","page":"Internal API","text":"struct BispacedMeasure <: BATMeasure\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nA measure in its primary space, together with an optional representation of it in a transformed space.\n\nConstructors:\n\nBispacedMeasure(main::BATMeasure)  # no transformed representation\nBispacedMeasure(f_transform, main)  # transformed side generated via f_transform\nBispacedMeasure(main::BATMeasure, transformed::Union{BATMeasure,Nothing}, f_hash::UInt)\n\nAs a measure, a BispacedMeasure behaves like its main side. The pair itself does not identify the transformed space, that meaning comes from the transform_intent of the EvaluatedMeasure the pair is part of.\n\nImplementation\n\nf_hash is the hash of the transformation function that produced the transformed side. It acts as a cheap compatibility witness when pairs are adopted into or consumed from an EvaluatedMeasure: a non-matching hash results in an error, never in silently wrong content. UInt(0) means that no claim is made, either because there is no transformed side or because the claim was invalidated. The witness only covers the connection between the two sides, supplying a fitting main side is the responsibility of the supplier.\n\nhash may be specialized for transformation function types to make the witness value-based instead of object-based. Without such a specialization the fallback is objectid, so stamps do not survive serialization or a new session. This errs on the safe side, since on a mismatch the transformed content can simply be re-derived. The witness is a strong practical guard, not a proof of identity: a hash collision could in principle let incompatible content pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.BispacedMeasure"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.pathfinder_gaussian_fit","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.pathfinder_gaussian_fit(\n    f_logd::Function, x0::AbstractVector{<:Real},\n    optalg, context::BATContext;\n    history_length::Integer = 6, ndraws_elbo::Integer = 5\n)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nRuns single-path Pathfinder (Zhang et al. (2022)) from x0 and returns the mean μ, dense covariance Σ and elbo of the maximum-ELBO local Gaussian approximation of the target along an L-BFGS trajectory, as a NamedTuple, or nothing if no approximation with a finite ELBO is found.\n\nThe trajectory is generated by maximizing the log-density function f_logd via maximize_density with optalg, which must be a gradient-based backend that records iterates and gradients (e.g. an OptimAlg with Optim.LBFGS). Optimizer failures count as path-local failures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.pathfinder_gaussian_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.repetition_to_weights","page":"Internal API","text":"repetition_to_weights(v::AbstractVector)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nDrop (subsequently) repeated samples by adding weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.repetition_to_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.reweight_relative!","page":"Internal API","text":"reweight_relative!(stats, factor::Real)\n\nScale historical weights in BAT's internal online statistics. Zero empties all statistics and metadata; one is an identity. Positive factors scale first moments and centered scatter by factor, and squared weights by factor^2, preserving the chosen weight class's bias correction.\n\nRequires a finite nonnegative factor and gradual underflow. Positive factors are converted to each accumulator's precision. Unrepresentable conversion or scaling throws before any component is changed. Nonzero scaled moments, weight sums and scatter must remain in the normal range; compensated low parts may round away provided the represented first moment does not vanish. Callers must have exclusive ownership of stats.\n\nAuxiliary log-density moments in MCMC statistics may remain subnormal, but must stay finite and must not vanish when nonzero. Their weight sums and squared weights retain the normal-range requirement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.reweight_relative!"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.smallest_credible_intervals","page":"Internal API","text":"smallest_credible_intervals(X, W = UnitWeights(...); p = nothing,\n    nsigma_equivalent = nothing, mode = :disjoint)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturn empirical credible intervals. Use :connected for the shortest single interval. Set p in (0, 1] or nsigma_equivalent; the default is one sigma. Log-weight rounding may conservatively widen intervals.\n\n\n\n\n\nsmallest_credible_intervals(smpl::DensitySampleVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}; kwargs...)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.smallest_credible_intervals"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.sum_first_dim","page":"Internal API","text":"@propagate_inbounds sum_first_dim(A::AbstractArray, j::Integer, ks::Integer...)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nCalculate the equivalent of sum(A[:, j, ks...]).\n\n\n\n\n\n@propagate_inbounds sum_first_dim(A::AbstractArray)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nIf A is a vector, return sum(A), else sum(A, 1)[:].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.sum_first_dim"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.supports_rand","page":"Internal API","text":"supports_rand(m)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nCheck whether a measure-like object m supports rand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.supports_rand"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.transform_function","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.transform_function(intent::TransformIntent, object)\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nReturn the transformation function that intent implies for object.\n\nA TransformIntent, together with an object to be transformed, implies a concrete transformation function; the same intent and object always yield the same transformation. Methods of transform_function must derive the transformation from the intent and the object alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.transform_function"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.trunc_logpdf_ratio","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.trunc_logpdf_ratio(orig_dist::Distribution{TP}, trunc_dist::Distribution{TP})::AbstractFloat\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nComputes the log-ratio between the amplitude of the PDF of a truncated distribution and the original (untruncted) distribution, within the support of the truncated one.\n\nThe PDF of both distributions must have the same shape within the support of trunc_dist and may only differ in amplitude.\n\nMainly used to implement BAT.truncate_batmeasure, in conjunction with BAT.truncate_dist_hard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.trunc_logpdf_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"internal_api/#BAT.truncate_dist_hard","page":"Internal API","text":"BAT.truncate_dist_hard(dist::Distribution{Univariate}, bounds::Interval)::Distribution{Univariate}\nBAT.truncate_dist_hard(dist::Distribution{Multivariate}, bounds::AbstractArray{<:Interval})::Distribution{Multivariate}\n\nBAT-internal, not part of stable public API.\n\nGeneralized variant of Distributions.truncated - also handles multivariate distributions and operates on a best-effort basis: If distributions cannot be truncated, may return the original distribution.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple\n\n    (dist = trunc_dist, logweight = logweight)\n\nwith the truncated distribution and the log-PDF amplitude difference to the original (see BAT.trunc_logpdf_ratio).\n\nMainly used to implement BAT.truncate_batmeasure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BAT.truncate_dist_hard"}]
}
